{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab C12 text: 'Proposition 7.4 (Answer invariance and the participation rule). B_i(r_i) is well defined, computable from the deliberative record together with the known structure (Theta, u_i), and invariant across the elements of R(P_i, r_i): every Lambda_i-measurable rule is implementable after any successful production, and the maximand of equation (6) makes no reference to which repair arrives. Moreover B_i(r_i) >= 0. Electing inquiry has represented value lambda_i B_i(r_i) - gamma_i, so agent i elects if and only if lambda_i B_i(r_i) >= gamma_i; in particular, elections occur only at dissonant records that admit some repair. Finally, B_i(r_i) > 0 if and only if some pinned rule strictly and robustly improves on the carried-forward policy: questions whose every admissible answer supports current behavior are worthless at any positive cost, however much model multiplicity remains.' \u8594? 'Example 1 (continued, Section 8): At the discovery record (T = 2, diagonal states served), deterministic conflicting payoffs certify the two tokens as distinct states, and the best coherent grouping splits them. The fine optimal gain is 0 on the winning token and 1/2 on the losing token, so L_i^fine = 1/2, L_i^cur = 0, J_i = 1/2 and B_hat_i = (2/2)*(1/2) = 1/2 for both firms. Each elects iff lambda_i^(0)*B_hat_i >= gamma_i, i.e. lambda_i^(0)/2 >= gamma_i. After the row refinement, the post-refinement horizon gain is exactly 1/2, so the firm re-differentiates when kappa_i < 1/2 and holds its old policy (enlightened inertia) when kappa_i >= 1/2. In a later stretch serving the two off-diagonal states, firm 1 holds the row refinement and firm 2 the column refinement. Then L_1^fine = L_1^cur = J_1 = B_hat_1 = 0 (the firm holding the truth never inquires again), while L_2^fine = 1, L_2^cur = 0, J_2 = 1 and B_hat_2 = (2/4)*1 = 1/2. The winner/loser asymmetry B_hat_1 = 0 < B_hat_2 is the durable point.'\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab C12 condition: ['B_i(r_i) is defined by equation (6) under the subjective recurrence premise at a dissonant record with R(P_i, r_i) != emptyset, and B_i(r_i) = 0 otherwise', 'gamma_i > 0', 'lambda_i > 0', 'lambda_i <= 1', 'production is elective and dissonance-triggered (Assumption 7.1)'] \u8594? None\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab C12 main: True \u8594? False\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab C12 evidence pointer: 'paper/paper.md#proof-prop-participation' \u8594? 'paper/paper.md:L842-L870'\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab C13 text: "Example 1 (continued, section 7). At the dissonant record both firms' committed constant policies disagree with exactly one revealed label (firm 1 lost at s11, firm 2 at s00). Every restorative refinement separates the two labeled tokens, and implementing a token's revealed label at its recurrence gains exactly one-half against every rival action: if the rival plays the winning architecture the switch converts a differentiated loss (0) into a pooled half; if the rival switches, it converts a pooled half into a differentiated win (1). Hence B_i = 1/2 for both firms, and each elects inquiry if and only if lambda_i/2 >= gamma_i; the valuation uses no conjecture about the rival and no reference to which of the four admissible refinements might arrive, Theta_i(t) is a singleton, and the bound is exact. After a validation generation in which firm 1 has acquired the row refinement (certain of the true row assignment) and firm 2 the column refinement (certain of the false column assignment), their policies differ at the two off-diagonal states, firm 1 wins both, and the labels revealed there falsify firm 2's assignment: B_1 = 0, so the firm holding the truth never inquires again and its awareness remains permanently incomplete; B_2 = 1, so firm 2 elects whenever lambda_2 >= gamma_2 and, upon success, is driven stepwise to complete awareness." \u8594? "Theorem 9.2 (No additional evidence beyond the generating record): Conditional on the analyst record r_bar_i and the pre-production inquiry history h_i, a record-measurable, truth-blind production, formulation, and bridge-prior-selection rule makes the complete output O_i = (P_i', m', mu_i^+) conditionally independent of the candidate objective assignment. Consequently, under the reflective joint law, for K_i(.|r_bar_i, h_i)-almost every realized output o and every tau' in calT, nu(tau' | r_bar_i, h_i, O_i = o) = nu(tau' | r_bar_i, h_i), and therefore W(o) = nu(A_o | r_bar_i, h_i). Passing the repair test adds no information beyond the record. If candidates assign equal likelihood to (r_bar_i, h_i), their posterior odds remain their reflective prior odds."\par}
