{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -## A. Proofs of Theorem [9.4](../paper/paper.md#thm-absorption) and Proposition [9.5](../paper/paper.md#prop-rest-points) \{#app-absorption\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +## A. The evidence process \{#app-eprocess\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix agent $i$ and theory $m$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At a visited pair $(C,a)$ write $p=p_i^\{*\}(\\cdot\\mid C,a)$ and $q=\\marg\{i\}m(C)(a)$; adequacy at $(C,a)$ is $\\TV(p,q)\\le\\eta$, equivalently $\\lvert p(D)-q(D)\\rvert\\le\\eta$ for every $D\\subseteq X_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For each such $D$ and each betting fraction $\\lambda$, retain the one-sided factor\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +F_\{\\lambda,D\}(x)=1+\\lambda\\bigl(\\mathbf 1\\\{x\\in D\\\}-(q(D)+\\eta)\\bigr).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix $0<\\lambda_\{\\max\}\\le1/(1+\\eta)$, choose a countable dense set $\\Lambda\\subset(0,\\lambda_\{\\max\})$, and assign every $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ a strictly positive atomic weight, with the weights summing to one.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Assign every $D\\subseteq X_i$ strictly positive weight as well, and let $M_i^\{m,(C,a)\}$ be the resulting convex mixture of the fixed processes that multiply by $F_\{\\lambda,D\}(x)$ at each visit to $(C,a)$ and by $1$ otherwise.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because $D$ ranges over all subsets of $X_i$, a negative discrepancy on $D$ is a positive discrepancy on $D^c$, so no separate sign mixture is needed.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under adequacy, $p(D)\\le q(D)+\\eta$ and hence $\\mathbb E_p[F_\{\\lambda,D\}]\\le1$, so every fixed component is a nonnegative supermartingale and their convex mixture remains one.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Assign every visited-pair component a strictly positive weight and set\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +E_i^\{m\}=\\sum_\{(C,a)\}v_\{(C,a)\}\\,M_i^\{m,(C,a)\},\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\textstyle\\sum_\{(C,a)\}v_\{(C,a)\}=1,\\ v_\{(C,a)\}>0.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +As a convex combination of nonnegative supermartingales, $E_i^\{m\}$ is a nonnegative supermartingale under the null that $m$ is adequate at every pair, with $E_i^\{m\}=1$ before play; Ville's inequality then gives $\\Pr(\\sup_t E_i^\{m\}\\ge e^\{b_i\})\\le e^\{-b_i\}$, the false-alarm bound of [definition 6.1](../paper/paper.md#def-test).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If instead $m$ is strictly inadequate at a recurrent pair $j^\{*\}=(C,a)$, some $D$ satisfies $p(D)>q(D)+\\eta$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For that event define\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +g(\\lambda)=\\mathbb E_p\\log\\!\\left(1+\\lambda[\\mathbf 1_D-(q(D)+\\eta)]\\right).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Then $g(0)=0$ and $g'(0)=p(D)-q(D)-\\eta>0$, so every sufficiently small positive $\\lambda$ has $g(\\lambda)>0$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Density of $\\Lambda$ supplies a positive-weight atom $\\lambda^\{*\}$ with strictly positive expected log-growth; if $W^\{*\}$ is its fixed component and $\\pi^\{*\}>0$ its weight within the pair mixture, the strong law under recurrent visitation gives $W^\{*\}(t)\\to\\infty$ almost surely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because the global process assigns the pair fixed weight $v_\{j^\{*\}\}>0$, pathwise\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +E_i^\{m\}(t)\\ge v_\{j^\{*\}\}\\pi^\{*\}W^\{*\}(t)\\longrightarrow\\infty\\quad\\text\{a.s.\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The positive per-period log-growth rate of such a component is its *betting exponent* (and the supremum across available components is the optimal log-growth exponent); this is the dominating-component property used in [lemma D.1](#lem-test-consistency) and [proposition D.7](#prop-capacity).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The exact deterministic case $\\eta=0$ uses a separate zero-likelihood detector rather than the stochastic mixture above.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Conceptually, $E_i^m(t)=1$ until the first observed consequence to which theory $m$ assigns probability zero, at which point $E_i^m(t)=+\\infty$ and remains there.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A $0$-adequate deterministic theory never encounters such an event and is never falsely rejected, whereas a $0$-inadequate theory at a recurrent pair encounters a diagnostic impossible consequence almost surely and is rejected at its first occurrence.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Thus $b_i$ has no effect in the deterministic case.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
