{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Per-token gain and deliberative leverage \{#per-token-gain-and-deliberative-leverage\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Consider a retained token whose coherent group pins mechanism $\\theta^\{a^\{*\}\}$ while the standing policy plays $a\\neq a^\{*\}$ at the token's cell, at $\\beta=1$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Proposing $a^\{*\}$: if the rival plays $a^\{*\}$, the standing action yields $0$ (differentiated loss) and $a^\{*\}$ yields $\\tfrac12$ (pool); if the rival plays $a$, the standing action yields $\\tfrac12$ (pool) and $a^\{*\}$ yields $1$ (differentiated win).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The per-token robust gain is $\\tfrac12$ in both cases\u8212?transparency makes $\\Theta_i(g)$ a singleton, so the mechanism minimum is degenerate.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A token whose group's optimal action equals the standing one contributes $0$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At discovery, the winning token contributes zero and the losing token contributes $\\tfrac12$ to the fine value, while under the coarse one-cell current language a uniform reversal gains $\\tfrac12$ on the losing token and loses $\\tfrac12$ on the winning token.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Thus\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L^\{\\mathrm\{fine\}\}=\\tfrac12,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L^\{\\mathrm\{cur\}\}=0,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +J=\\tfrac12,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat B=(2/2)(\\tfrac12)=\\tfrac12.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +After the validation stretch of [section 8](../paper/paper.md#sec-economics) (serving the two off-diagonal states, valuation horizon $T=2$), the later winner has\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L_1^\{\\mathrm\{fine\}\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L_1^\{\\mathrm\{cur\}\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +J_1\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat B_1\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=0.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The later loser's four tokens split into two pinned-mechanism groups; two disagree with its column policy and contribute $\\tfrac12$ each under fine contingency, while two agree and contribute zero.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Within either current column a uniform reversal gains $\\tfrac12$ on one token and loses $\\tfrac12$ on the other, so\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L_2^\{\\mathrm\{fine\}\}=1,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L_2^\{\\mathrm\{cur\}\}=0,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +J_2=1,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat B_2=(2/4)1=\\tfrac12.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The durable content is the asymmetry $\\widehat B_1=0<\\widehat B_2$, not the coincidence that $\\widehat B_2$ equals the discovery value.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ### The trap and its knife edge \{#the-trap-and-its-knife-edge\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -With both firms certain of the column assignment, the column policy pools at\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -every state; every payoff is $\\tfrac12$; and under pooled play every\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -mechanism in $\\\{\\theta^0,\\theta^1,\\theta^n\\\}$ consistent with the column\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -labels predicts $\\tfrac12$ at $\\beta=1$.  No episode is labeled, active sets\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -never empty, and $B_i=0$; conditions (i)\u8211?(iv) of [definition 9.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue) hold.  For\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\beta<1$, at an off-diagonal state the pooled action is objectively wrong,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -realized payoffs are $\\beta/2$ against a prediction of $\\tfrac12$, and the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -payoff level itself reveals the label (payoff $\\tfrac12$ implies the pooled\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -architecture is preferred; $\\beta/2$ implies the other is).  The revealed\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -label conflicts with a diagonal label inside one column cell, the active set\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -empties, the episode enters the disagreement record, and the reflective\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -posterior over the row and column worlds becomes degenerate: dissonance,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -leverage, and warrant closure arrive together.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With both firms certain of the column assignment, the column policy pools at every state; every payoff is $\\tfrac12$; and under pooled play every mechanism consistent with the column cells predicts $\\tfrac12$ at $\\beta=1$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +No token conflicts, active sets never empty, and $\\widehat B_i=0$; conditions (i)\u8211?(iv) of [definition 10.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue) hold.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For $\\beta<1$, at an off-diagonal state the pooled action is objectively wrong and the realized payoff is $\\beta/2$ against a prediction of $\\tfrac12$; the payoff $\\beta/2$ uniquely reveals that the other architecture is preferred, while a payoff of $\\tfrac12$ would leave preferred and neutral both possible.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The revealed conflict makes one column cell incoherent, the active set empties, and the reflective posterior over the row and column worlds becomes degenerate: dissonance, leverage, and warrant closure arrive together.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ### Persistence payoffs \{#persistence-payoffs\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -With firm 1 playing the true row assignment and firm 2 a constant\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -architecture, the firms pool at two states and differentiate at two, firm 1\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -winning both differentiated states: average payoffs $\\tfrac34$ and\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\tfrac14$ under uniform $\\rho$.  Firm 2's record rejects all three constant\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theories (it wins nothing differentiated and loses twice), so it sits in\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -dissonance with $B_2>0$; stasis obtains whenever\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\gamma_2>\\lambda_2B_2$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Take $\\beta<1$, firm 1 playing the true row assignment, firm 2 coarse and playing a constant architecture $c$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The firms differentiate at the two states of the row that $c$ misserves\u8212?firm 1 wins both\u8212?and pool at the other row's two states, giving firm 1 average payoff $\\tfrac34$ and firm 2 $\\tfrac14$ under uniform $\\rho$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Firm 2's cumulative record rejects all three constant theories: its differentiated losses defeat the constant theory matching $c$, its pooled payoff of one-half defeats the opposite theory's $\\beta/2$ prediction, and neutrality fails at the differentiated losses, so it is genuinely dissonant.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For the balanced four-token record, the two correctly served tokens contribute zero and the two misserved tokens contribute $\\tfrac12$ each, so $L_2^\{\\mathrm\{fine\}\}=1$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A uniform current-language reversal yields $\\beta-1<0$, while retaining the standing action yields zero, so\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +L_2^\{\\mathrm\{cur\}\}=0,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +J_2=1,\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat B_2=(2/4)1=\\tfrac12.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For other realized compositions its deliberative leverage is positive whenever the representational increment is positive and remains bounded as in [proposition 8.4](../paper/paper.md#prop-participation).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Permanent stasis requires\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\gamma_2\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +>\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\lambda_2^\{(k_\{2,t_0\})\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat B_2(r_\{2,t\})\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\qquad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\text\{for every \\(t\\ge t_0\\) along the induced continuation;\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +the record-uniform condition\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\gamma_2\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +>\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\lambda_2^\{(k_\{2,t_0\})\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +T\\bigl(\\max\\pi_2-\\min\\pi_2\\bigr)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +is sufficient.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At $\\beta=1$, the same initial constant-$c$ record leaves only the opposite constant theory active.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A low switching hurdle induces one reversal, after which evidence generated under the reversed policy rejects that last theory and permanent no-reentry makes the firm dissonant; a sufficiently high hurdle instead permits policy inertia under the switching rule.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The process does not oscillate indefinitely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +## D. Deferred formal results \{#app-deferred\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This appendix collects supporting results whose formal statements were moved out of the main text; statements and proofs are unchanged.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The following lemma establishes the consistency of the sequential adequacy test of [definition 6.1](../paper/paper.md#def-test).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Lemma D.1 (Test consistency) \{#lem-test-consistency\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix a partition $\\mathcal P_i$ and a standing profile, and suppose every theory is either $\\eta$-adequate or strictly inadequate at each visited pair (none sits exactly at the adequacy boundary).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Let $t_0$ denote the beginning of the stable standing-profile regime after a finite prefix, and let $E_i^m(t_0)$ be the inherited evidence value of a theory $m$ active at $t_0$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because theories inactive at initialization never re-enter, sequential rejection is permanent, and the theories are finite, the active set is monotone and eventually constant; and almost surely:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (a) every theory inactive at $t_0$ remains absent, and every theory active at $t_0$ that is strictly inadequate at a recurrent post-$t_0$ pair is eventually rejected and absent from the active set; and\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (b) a theory active at $t_0$ and $\\eta$-adequate at every recurrent post-$t_0$ pair has conditional probability of future false rejection at most $E_i^m(t_0)e^\{-b_i\}$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Active status implies $E_i^m(t_0)<e^\{b_i\}$, so every theory in (b) has strictly positive conditional probability of surviving forever.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Consequently, if every expressible theory falls under (a), eventual dissonance occurs almost surely; if at least one recurrently adequate theory remains active at $t_0$, eventual dissonance is not an almost-sure consequence; and if one such theory satisfies $E_i^m(t_0)\\le1$, dissonance is avoided with probability at least $1-e^\{-b_i\}$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proof \{#proof-lem-test-consistency\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A recurrent pair is visited each period with a fixed positive probability under the i.i.d. full-support draw, hence infinitely often; a frozen pair is visited only in the finite prefix and affects the inherited value $E_i^m(t_0)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If a theory $m$ active at $t_0$ is strictly inadequate at a recurrent pair $j^\{*\}$, [appendix A](#app-eprocess) supplies a fixed betting component $W^\{*\}$ for that pair with strictly positive expected log-growth.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The pair mixture gives $W^\{*\}$ positive weight $\\pi^\{*\}$ and the global evidence process gives the pair positive weight $v_\{j^\{*\}\}$, so pathwise $E_i^m(t)\\ge v_\{j^\{*\}\}\\pi^\{*\}W^\{*\}(t)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The strong law gives $W^\{*\}(t)\\to\\infty$ almost surely, hence $E_i^m(t)\\to\\infty$ almost surely; therefore $\\log E_i^m(t)$ eventually crosses $b_i$, and $m$ is rejected.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If $m$ is active at $t_0$ and $\\eta$-adequate at every recurrent post-$t_0$ pair, then $(E_i^m(t))_\{t\\ge t_0\}$ is a nonnegative supermartingale and conditional Ville's inequality gives\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\Pr\\!\\left(\\sup_\{t\\ge t_0\}E_i^m(t)\\ge e^\{b_i\}\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathcal F_\{t_0\}\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\le E_i^m(t_0)e^\{-b_i\}.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +No re-entry, permanence of sequential rejection, and finiteness of $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ make the active set monotone and eventually constant; collecting the two cases gives the dissonance dichotomy. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The next proposition shows that the repair test of [definition 7.1](../paper/paper.md#def-restorative) factorizes cell by cell.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition D.2 (Cellwise coherence) \{#prop-coherence\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For a candidate refinement $\\mathcal P_i\\prec\\mathcal P'$, define\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\Theta_i^\{R\}(C'\\mid\\bar r_i)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\left\\\{\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\theta\\in\\Theta:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\begin\{aligned\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +&\\TV\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +  \\hat p_i(\\cdot\\mid C',a),\\marg\{i\}\\theta(a)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 + \\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 + \\leq \\eta+w\\!\\left(n_i(C',a)\\right)\\\\[-0.2em]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +&\\hspace\{7em\}\\text\{for every $a$ with \}n_i(C',a)>0\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\end\{aligned\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right\\\}.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Call $C'$ *repair-coherent* if $\\Theta_i^\{R\}(C'\\mid\\bar r_i)\\neq\\emptysetset$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Then $\\mathcal P'$ is restorative if and only if every cell of $\\mathcal P'$ is repair-coherent.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proof \{#proof-prop-coherence\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because $M(\\mathcal P')=\\Theta^\{\\mathcal P'\}$ and every constraint in equation (6) involves only the mechanism assigned to one cell,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P',\\bar r_i)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\prod_\{C'\\in\\mathcal P'\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\Theta_i^\{R\}(C'\\mid\\bar r_i).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This product is nonempty exactly when every factor is nonempty. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The following remark records when a dissonant record admits no expressible repair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Remark D.3 (Irreparable records) \{#rem-irreparable\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With deterministic mechanisms, every dissonant record is repairable.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under the exact calibration $\\eta=0$ and $w\\equiv0$, consider the singleton partition of $S$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At each state $s$ and each observed profile $a$, every retained consequence was generated by the deterministic mechanism $\\tau(s)$; hence $\\tau(s)$ fits all observations recoded to $\\\{s\\\}$ exactly.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The objective assignment therefore belongs to the repair-consistent active set on the singleton partition.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If the current partition is already the singleton partition, dissonance is impossible; otherwise finiteness of $\\Pi(S)$ ensures a minimally restorative refinement.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With stochastic mechanisms this guarantee fails.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Multiple observations at a single state may yield empirical conditionals for which no one mechanism satisfies equation (6) across the observed profiles.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because no refinement can split a state, such a record has $\\mathcal R(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)=\\emptysetset$ and is *irreparable*: dissonance names a defect that no expressible refinement can repair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Irreparability is an analyst-level property: it is a fact about $\\bar r_i$, and the agent, lacking the state labels, cannot in general tell an irreparable record from a repairable one\u8212?this is precisely the agent's inability to distinguish causal heterogeneity from mechanism noise, developed in [section 8](../paper/paper.md#sec-economics).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +When the record is irreparable the technology fails surely ([definition 7.2](../paper/paper.md#def-technology)); the resulting behavior is governed by the deliberative valuation and failure-discouragement of [section 8](../paper/paper.md#sec-economics).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If the deliberative record still admits a positive-leverage coherent grouping, the agent may elect a doomed inquiry until its achievability weight decays; if no coherent grouping prices the question, it declines immediately.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Neither response requires the agent to recognize analyst-level irreparability.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under recurring visitation irreparability is typically transient, but a record whose critical pairs stop being visited can remain irreparable forever, a possibility [section 10](../paper/paper.md#sec-dynamics) classifies within dissonant stasis.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The following remark records what deterministic mechanisms certify about deliberative leverage, and the record-level transparency condition under which the estimate is exact.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Remark D.4 (What determinism certifies\u8212?and what it does not) \{#rem-certification\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Determinism supplies a limited but useful separability certificate.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Two tokens sharing the same observed pair $(C,a)$ but differing in consequence cannot have arisen from the same state, because a state and action profile fix the consequence.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Any restorative refinement must therefore separate such a certified conflict.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Determinism alone does not, however, make every deliberative grouping realizable.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Tokens observed under different action profiles can be grouped coherently even when their underlying particulars cannot be separated in the proposed way.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In general, $\\widehat B_i$ therefore remains a deliberative estimate even with deterministic mechanisms.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A stronger, record-level condition restores exactness.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In the deterministic zero-tolerance case with $\\bar n=1$ and $w\\equiv0$, call a record *pointwise transparent* if $\\Theta_i(\\\{t\\\})$ is a singleton for every retained token $t$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Every token pins one mechanism, and every restorative refinement separates tokens carrying incompatible pinned mechanisms.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A fine-contingent proposal chooses actions separately at each current-cell/pinned-group intersection, so it is implementable under any restorative refinement; a coherent group may span current cells without merging them because every new partition refines the current one.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The current-language benchmark, which chooses one action per current cell, is already implementable.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Consequently both value terms, and therefore their representational increment, are exact and repair-invariant.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The running example's differentiated discovery record is pointwise transparent, so its leverage calculation has precisely this property.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under stochastic mechanisms, differing consequences at one $(C,a)$-pair may reflect mechanism noise rather than distinct states, and even a coherent grouping need not be realizable.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +There $\\widehat B_i$ is genuinely an estimate, and the achievability weight prices the possibility that no arriving distinction delivers it.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Counterfactual transparency\u8212?a small $\\Theta_i(g)$, ideally a singleton\u8212?makes the estimate sharper; opacity enlarges $\\Theta_i(g)$, lowers robust leverage, and can rationally suppress inquiry.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The next proposition characterizes rest points of the dynamics of [section 10](../paper/paper.md#sec-dynamics); its proof is in [appendix B](#app-absorption).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition D.5 (Rest points and persistence) \{#prop-rest-points\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (i) Suppose awareness partitions and standing policies are eventually constant almost surely, conjectures and reference posteriors converge, and each non-stasis agent's standing policy is a strict no-switch point at the limit\u8212?either its switching gain is bounded strictly below $\\kappa_i$, or, when $\\kappa_i=0$, the standing action is the unique $U_i$-maximizer so the gain is exactly zero.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +  Then the limiting configuration is a *partial SCUE*: the SCUE conditions hold for every non-stasis agent, taking the stasis agents' constant carried-forward policies as fixed environment.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +  (Belief coordinates need not literally freeze; the strict slack transfers the no-switch condition to the limit by continuity of the finite-dimensional payoff comparison.)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (ii) Conversely, consider a configuration whose epistemic states satisfy the SCUE conditions, whose play generates only records with $\\widehat B_i=0$, and in which each agent's standing action at each cell is strictly optimal under every conjecture and every theory in its active set.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (a) With deterministic mechanisms, the configuration persists: dissonance, elections, and policy changes never occur, and the SCUE conditions continue to hold, with conjectures converging empirically to induced play.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (b) With stochastic mechanisms, suppose in addition that, at the entry configuration, every agent's active set is nonempty, every theory it contains is $\\eta$-adequate under the configuration's play and carries no accumulated evidence against it ($E_i^\{m\}\\le1$, automatic when the configuration is entered on a freshly refined language), and the standing action is strictly optimal under every conjecture and every $\\eta$-adequate theory.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    Then, conditional on the entry history, with probability at least $1-\\sum_i e^\{-b_i\}$, no agent is ever dissonant, no switch or election occurs, and the SCUE conditions hold in every subsequent period, with conjectures converging empirically to induced play.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    The residual $\\sum_i e^\{-b_i\}$ is the sequential test's false-alarm bound: it shrinks as dissonance capacities rise, and vanishes in the deterministic case\u8212?so in stochastic environments *capacity governs the robustness of a rest point*.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The proof is in [appendix B](#app-absorption).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The next proposition locates where error can survive in a SCUE.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition D.6 (On-path adequacy, and where error lives) \{#prop-adequacy\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In a SCUE, acting-belief mass on every $\\eta$-inadequate theory vanishes almost surely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under the strict-adequacy condition of [lemma D.1](#lem-test-consistency), or in its exact deterministic case, every theory remaining in the eventual support of $\\mu_i$ is $\\eta$-adequate at every on-path pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +All falsity retaining nonvanishing acting-belief mass is confined to what the configuration does not expose: mechanism assignments at states pooled with causally different states whose mixture remains within tolerance of a single mechanism at the visited profiles, and counterfactual claims at unvisited profiles.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proof \{#proof-prop-adequacy\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The first claim is [definition 10.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue)(iii).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under strict adequacy, or under the lemma's exact deterministic argument, [lemma D.1](#lem-test-consistency) makes the active set eventually constant and excludes every $\\eta$-inadequate theory; judged updating restricts the acting belief to that set.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The residual clause is the complement of the on-path constraints. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The next proposition gives the comparative statics of dissonance capacity.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition D.7 (Capacity: detection speed and robustness) \{#prop-capacity\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix agent $i$ and stochastic mechanisms satisfying [assumption 6.2](../paper/paper.md#ass-full-support).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (i) *Detection.* Fix an exposed standing profile and suppose the evidence processes are freshly initialized when that profile begins.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +  For each theory $m$, let $d_i^m$ be the supremum of the positive per-period expected log-growth rates available from the fixed betting components associated with its recurrent inadequate pairs in [appendix A](#app-eprocess), and define $D_i=\\min_m d_i^m>0$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +  The expected number of periods until agent $i$'s dissonance satisfies $\\mathbb E[\\tau_\{\\mathrm\{dissonance\}\}]=O(b_i/D_i)$ and $\\mathbb E[\\tau_\{\\mathrm\{dissonance\}\}]=\\Omega(b_i)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +- (ii) *Robustness.* At a non-exposed stochastic profile entered under the hypotheses of [proposition D.5](#prop-rest-points)(ii-b), agent $i$ spuriously becomes dissonant with probability at most $e^\{-b_i\}$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In stochastic environments, $b_i$ creates a detection\u8211?robustness tradeoff: a larger $b_i$ lowers false-alarm risk but requires more accumulated evidence to detect a broken language.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under deterministic $\\eta=0$ mechanisms, a recurrently inadequate theory is instead rejected at its first diagnostic impossible consequence, so detection speed is governed by the occurrence probability of diagnostic evidence rather than by $b_i$; adequate theories have zero false-alarm probability, and capacity affects neither detection nor robustness in the exact deterministic model.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proof \{#proof-prop-capacity\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +(i) Exposure makes $d_i^m>0$ for every expressible theory and finiteness makes $D_i>0$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because the countable betting support need not attain the supremum, choose for each $m$ a recurrent inadequate pair $j_m$ and a positive-weight component $W_m$ at that pair whose per-period expected log-growth is at least $d_i^m/2$, and let $\\pi_m$ be its within-pair mixture weight.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Component domination gives $E_i^m(t)\\ge v_\{j_m\}\\pi_m W_m(t)$ for fixed positive weights $v_\{j_m\}\\pi_m$, so the rejection time $\\tau_m$ is no later than the crossing time of $\\log W_m$ above $b_i-\\log(v_\{j_m\}\\pi_m)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The stochastic construction has bounded log-increments; standard bounded-increment stopping-time and Wald reasoning therefore gives $\\mathbb E[\\tau_m]\\le 2(b_i+c_m)/d_i^m$, where $c_m$ is independent of $b_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Since $\\tau_\{\\mathrm\{dissonance\}\}=\\max_m\\tau_m\\le\\sum_m\\tau_m$ and the theory set is finite, $\\mathbb E[\\tau_\{\\mathrm\{dissonance\}\}]=O(b_i/D_i)$ with constants independent of $b_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Conversely, bounded one-period growth of each freshly initialized stochastic evidence process implies that no process can cross $e^\{b_i\}$ before a number of periods proportional to $b_i$, yielding $\\mathbb E[\\tau_\{\\mathrm\{dissonance\}\}]=\\Omega(b_i)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Part (ii) is the per-agent bound of [proposition D.5](#prop-rest-points)(ii-b); neither rate argument applies to the separate exact deterministic detector. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The following definition and proposition formalize diagnostic environments and their exclusion of payoff-relevant policy error in SCUE.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Definition D.8 (Payoff-relevant policy error and diagnostic environments) \{#def-diagnostic\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix a profile of epistemic states inducing standing profile $\\sigma$, and write\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +a^\{\\sigma\}(s)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\bigl(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\sigma_j(C_\{\\mathcal P_j\}(s))\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\bigr)_\{j\\in N\}.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$'s standing policy has a *payoff-relevant policy error* at state $s$ if\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim\\tau(s)(a^\{\\sigma\}(s))\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\!\\left[\\pi_i(x_i)\\right]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +<\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\max_\{\\widetilde a_i\\in A_i\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\tau(s)(\\widetilde a_i,a_\{-i\}^\{\\sigma\}(s))\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\!\\left[\\pi_i(x_i)\\right].\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Thus objective evaluation makes the standing action strictly suboptimal, rather than merely changing the set of optimal actions while leaving the standing action optimal.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The primitives are *diagnostic at tolerance $\\eta$* if, for every profile of epistemic states satisfying the strategic stationarity and conjecture-confirmation conditions *(i)*\u8211?*(ii)* of [definition 10.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue), any payoff-relevant policy error for any agent $i$ implies that $\\mathcal P_i$ is exposed under the induced standing profile in the sense of [definition 6.4](../paper/paper.md#def-exposure).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition D.9 (No payoff-relevant policy error in diagnostic environments) \{#prop-diagnostic\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In an environment diagnostic at tolerance $\\eta$, no SCUE contains a payoff-relevant policy error.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proof \{#proof-prop-diagnostic\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Suppose, to the contrary, that a SCUE contains a payoff-relevant policy error for agent $i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Every SCUE satisfies the strategic stationarity and conjecture-confirmation conditions *(i)*\u8211?*(ii)* of [definition 10.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue), so diagnosticity implies that $\\mathcal P_i$ is exposed under the induced standing profile.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Hence every theory in $M(\\mathcal P_i)$, and therefore every theory active on that language, is $\\eta$-inadequate at some recurrent on-path pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +By [lemma D.1](#lem-test-consistency), every such active theory is eventually rejected almost surely; theories inactive at initialization or already rejected cannot re-enter.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ is finite, the active set is therefore eventually empty almost surely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This contradicts condition *(iii)* of [definition 10.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue), which requires the active set to be almost surely eventually nonempty. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The next proposition gives a sufficient condition on primitives for diagnosticity.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition D.10 (Strict punishment implies diagnosticity) \{#prop-punishing\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Call the primitives *strictly punishing at tolerance $\\eta$* if, for every profile of epistemic states satisfying conditions *(i)*\u8211?*(ii)* of [definition 10.1](../paper/paper.md#def-scue), every payoff-relevant policy error for agent $i$ at state $s$ satisfies\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\min_\{m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\TV\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +p_i^\{*\}\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\cdot\\mid\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +C_\{\\mathcal P_i\}(s),a^\{\\sigma\}(s)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right),\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\marg\{i\}m(C_\{\\mathcal P_i\}(s))(a^\{\\sigma\}(s))\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +>\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\eta.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Strictly punishing primitives are diagnostic at tolerance $\\eta$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Consequently, every SCUE under strictly punishing primitives is free of payoff-relevant policy error.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proof \{#proof-prop-punishing\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At a payoff-relevantly erroneous state, the displayed inequality places the on-path conditional more than $\\eta$ from the prediction of every expressible theory at the induced recurrent cell\u8211?profile pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Every $m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)$ is therefore $\\eta$-inadequate, so $\\mathcal P_i$ is exposed.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because this implication holds for every profile satisfying the strategic stationarity and conjecture-confirmation conditions, the primitives are diagnostic by [definition D.8](#def-diagnostic).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The final claim follows from [proposition D.9](#prop-diagnostic). \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The concluding remark records the local robustness of strict margins.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Remark D.11 (Local robustness of strict margins) \{#rem-local-robustness\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix the awareness partitions, a standing profile $\\sigma$, and a state $s$ at which agent $i$ has a payoff-relevant policy error.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Let the strict objective payoff margin be\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\varepsilon\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\max_\{\\widetilde a_i\\in A_i\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\tau(s)(\\widetilde a_i,a_\{-i\}^\{\\sigma\}(s))\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +[\\pi_i(x_i)]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +-\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim\\tau(s)(a^\{\\sigma\}(s))\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +[\\pi_i(x_i)]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +>0,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +and let the strict diagnostic separation margin be\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\delta\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\min_\{m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\TV\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +p_i^\{*\}\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\cdot\\mid\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +C_\{\\mathcal P_i\}(s),a^\{\\sigma\}(s)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right),\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\marg\{i\}m(C_\{\\mathcal P_i\}(s))(a^\{\\sigma\}(s))\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +-\\eta\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +>0.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For a fixed finite indexing of states, actions, consequences, and mechanisms, and holding the induced profile and relevant support fixed, these payoff and total-variation comparisons are continuous in the underlying probability and payoff vectors.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Sufficiently small perturbations therefore preserve both strict margins.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This is a local stability result for a fixed profile and comparison, not a claim that global diagnosticity is an open property: perturbations may change which profiles satisfy the strategic stationarity and conjecture-confirmation conditions.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Likewise, concealment relative to a fixed profile is locally stable when some objectively false theory satisfies\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\max_\{(C,a)\\ \{\\rm visited\}\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\TV\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +p_i^\{*\}(\\cdot\\mid C,a),\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\marg\{i\}m(C)(a)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +<\\eta\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +with positive slack and the perturbation also preserves objective falsity.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A weakly concealing theory that binds at\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$\\TV=\\eta$ need not remain concealing after perturbation; exact matching at $\\eta=0$ is therefore a boundary case rather than an open region.\par}
