{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ## Abstract \{#abstract\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -The theory-based view treats economic actors as theorists whose causal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -understandings direct search, experimentation, and action.  Existing formal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -treatments begin after the theory exists.  This paper models where strategic\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theories come from.  Agents represent their environment through awareness\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -partitions of a state space; expressible causal theories assign elementary\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -mechanisms to awareness cells; theories and beliefs determine committed\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -policies; and the policy profile causes everyone's consequences through an\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -objective causal system.  Bayesian learning operates only within the current\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -language, and a misspecified posterior grows confident rather than alarmed,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -so detecting representational failure requires a distinct faculty: causal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -dissonance, the statistical rejection of every expressible theory.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Dissonance directs inquiry without determining its output; a costly, fallible\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -technology may produce a previously inexpressible distinction; and a bridge\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -prior restores Bayesian competence on the expanded language.  The value of a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -question is computable before its answers are conceivable, warrant is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -distinct from post-insight confidence\u8212?the insight itself is not\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -evidence\u8212?and an agent generally cannot measure its own overconfidence.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Because theories cause actions and actions generate evidence, inquiry dies\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -out almost surely, leaving every agent in a Bayesian world; the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -configurations that remain can preserve false theories, heterogeneous\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -awareness, and permanent performance differences whenever neither strategic\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -variety nor environmental harshness exposes them.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -**Keywords:** theory-based view; unawareness; insight; causal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -learning; subjective rationality; self-confirming equilibrium\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 + Where do strategic theories come from?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The theory-based view shows how causal theories direct managers' search, experimentation, and action, but its formal treatments begin after a theory\u8212?or at least a hypothesis space\u8212?exists.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This paper models the step before: how an actor whose causal language has failed comes to possess a new one.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Bayesian updating can rank the theories a manager can already express; it cannot produce a distinction never yet drawn, and confidence can grow even as every expressible theory fails.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +I model a separate, fallible faculty\u8212?causal dissonance\u8212?that detects when the represented class has broken, and a costly, elective inquiry process that may repair it.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Four results follow.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Experience directs repair without determining it, so insight is necessarily fallible.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A firm can rationally value a question before it can state any answer\u8212?and an unresolved action margin, not success, is what sustains that value.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The evidence that generates a theory cannot also validate it, which gives boards and investors a precise epistemic role.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +And because inquiry can die economically, firms facing identical environments can end with permanently different theories and payoffs: strategic heterogeneity can originate before priors.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +**Keywords:** theory-based view; unawareness; insight; causal learning; subjective rationality; self-confirming equilibrium\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ## 1. Introduction \{#sec-intro\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -The theory-based view of strategy holds that economic actors are theorists:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -managers and entrepreneurs carry causal accounts of how value is created, and\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -those accounts\u8212?not merely their information or their incentives\u8212?direct\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -what they notice, which experiments they run, and what they do\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -(Felin and Zenger 2017; Felin et al. 2024).  A decade of work has\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -established the consequences of holding a theory.  Theories guide\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -experimentation and search (Camuffo et al. 2020; Chavda et al. 2024),\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -support the acquisition of resources and rents (Ehrig and Zenger 2024), and\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -discipline entrepreneurial decision-making in the field\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -(Camuffo et al. 2024).  What this literature has not yet formalized is the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -step before all of that: where a genuinely new theory comes from.  Existing\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -formal treatments begin with the theory present\u8212?as a menu of candidate\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theories to select among (Camuffo et al. 2024), as a given causal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -conjecture that directs search (Chavda et al. 2024), or as a given\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -awareness advantage to be exploited competitively (Ehrig and Zenger 2024).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -The origin of the theory is described verbally and documented empirically\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -(Ott and Hannah 2024), and the absence of formal machinery for it has been\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -explicitly noted as the field's open analytical problem\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -(Felin et al. 2024).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -The difficulty is not a missing application of Bayesian reasoning; it is a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -boundary of Bayesian reasoning.  A Bayesian agent updates beliefs over a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -space of represented possibilities.  Conditioning reweights that space and\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -cannot enlarge it, so no sequence of updates makes an unrepresented causal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -distinction available for belief or action.  Worse, when every represented\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theory is false, the posterior does not sound an alarm: by the logic of\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -misspecified Bayesian learning (Berk 1966), it concentrates on the least\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -wrong of the available theories, and the agent grows more confident, not\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -less.  Within-language updating is self-sealing.  The economics of growing\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -awareness has developed axiomatic accounts of how beliefs should extend when\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -the state space expands (Karni and Vier\\o 2013; Karni and Vier\\o 2017; Vier\\o 2021), but\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -in those models the expansion itself arrives exogenously; models of discovery\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -in games describe how play can reveal objectively available actions\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -(Schipper 2021) without explaining how experience generates a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -substantive causal question or its answer.  What is missing, in both the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -strategy literature and the economics of unawareness, is a model of the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -transition: from a broken causal language, through a directed question, to a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -new representation, and back to ordinary Bayesian life.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -This paper models that transition, embedded in strategic interaction.  Its\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -organizing object is the chain\par}
