{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Existing work explains learning and action once theories, vocabularies, or awareness advantages exist; this paper supplies the pre-Bayesian passage from represented-class failure to a question, a fallible new representation, formulation, and renewed Bayesian judgment.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Its distinctive economic and epistemic results are that an agent can value a finer actionable distinction before representing its answers, and that evidence generating a truth-blind representation cannot be counted again as independent validation.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Embedding that passage in strategic interaction provides a mechanism by which play exposes or conceals coarse languages and establishes the existence of absorbing configurations with heterogeneous terminal languages and payoffs.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The contribution is therefore upstream: question histories and rational stopping points can generate strategic heterogeneity before priors over the resulting theories are available.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The four conversations meet there: theory-based strategy assumes a theory, unawareness research an expansion, causal learning a vocabulary, and subjective rationality a hypothesis space.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This paper endogenizes the strategically consequential transition into a new hypothesis space while leaving the semantic act that produces it primitive.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +## 4. The model \{#sec-model\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 4.1 Agents, states, actions, and consequences \{#sec-primitives\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +There are $n\\ge 2$ *agents*, $N=\\\{1,\\dots,n\\\}$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Time is a sequence of *periods* $t=1,2,\\dots$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In each period one state is drawn, every agent acts through a *standing policy* carried over from the previous period, and consequences are realized.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agents update beliefs every period.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If evidence mounts that their model of the world is in conflict with reality, they may engage in a process of costly inquiry which may then lead to refined awareness, changes to their held model, and potential revisions of their business strategy.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In each period a *state* $s\\in S$, with $S$ finite, is drawn independently from a full-support distribution $\\rho\\in\\Delta(S)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +States describe the strategically-relevant features of the environment that evolve or change over time: customer configurations, input conditions, competitive circumstances, available production technologies, and so on.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +(Their status in the agents' own representations is the subject of [section 4.3](#sec-awareness-sub).)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Contingent upon agent $i$'s awareness of the consequence-relevant features of his present state, he chooses an *action* $a_i$ from a finite set $A_i$ through the standing policy described below.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +An *action profile* is a list of each agent's action choice, $a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)$ where $A=\\prod_iA_i$ is the set of all action profiles.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Write $A_\{-i\}=\\prod_\{j\\neq i\}A_j$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Finally, $X=\\prod_iX_i$ is the finite set of performance-related *consequences* with typical element $x=(x_1,\\dots,x_n)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$'s consequence $x_i$ is everything a period delivers to it\u8212?profit, sales, rival behavior, and any other observed events.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Then, agent $i$'s *payoff function* $\\pi_i:X_i\\to\\mathbb R$ maps his consequences to payoffs.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 4.2 The objective causal system \{#sec-mechanisms-sub\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Causality is modeled in a general, high-level fashion.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Each period, the state $s$, together with the agents' actions $a$, determines the consequences $x$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 4.1 (Elementary mechanisms and the objective system) \{#def-mechanisms\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A finite set $\\Theta$ of *elementary causal mechanisms* is given, each element of which is a map $\\theta:A\\to\\Delta(X)$.[^2]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *objective assignment* is a function $\\tau:S\\to\\Theta$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Both $\\Theta$ and $\\tau$ are primitives of the model.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *objective causal system* is $\\tau(s)(a)\\in\\Delta(X)$, a distribution over consequences determined by the state and action profile associated with a period.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Two states $s$ and $s^\\prime$ are *causally equivalent* if $\\tau(s)=\\tau(s^\\prime)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Let $\\mathcal P^\{\\tau\}$ denote the partition of $S$ into causal-equivalence classes.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Finally, $\\marg\{i\}\\theta(a)$ denotes the $X_i$-marginal of $\\theta(a)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Thus, $\\tau$ implies a distribution of consequences for every action profile at every state, including profiles never played and states never occurring.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agents' actions, together with the state, cause their consequences.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A mechanism is the reduced form of whatever detailed model\u8212?a structural causal model of customer behavior, a market microstructure, a production process\u8212?underlies that relation; the paper imposes no structural-causal-model formalism, but examples may exhibit micro-foundations explicitly, as the running example's customer market does.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Three consequences of the causal reading recur.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +First, theories, defined next, are interventional objects: they make predictions at unplayed profiles.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Second, on-path data constrain $\\tau(s)(a)$ only at visited state\u8211?profile pairs, so observationally equivalent systems with different counterfactual content are generic; that is the engine of the identification results.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Third, the on-path distribution of consequences given an agent's category of states is a *selection* object\u8212?it mixes mechanisms according to which states co-occur with which play\u8212?and must never be conflated with any mechanism's own conditional.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### The Calibration Convention \{#conv-calibration\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Throughout, the *exact calibration* means deterministic mechanisms with adequacy tolerance $\\eta=0$ and finite-sample slack $w\\equiv0$ (the test calibration of [definition 6.1](#def-test)), in which tests reject at the first impossible observation and false alarms are zero; the running example uses it.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +All definitions are stated once for the general stochastic case; the exact calibration is the special case, and I flag the two places where it changes a conclusion: every dissonant record is repairable under the exact calibration but not in general ([remark D.3](../appendix/appendix.md#rem-irreparable)), and the dissonance capacity $b_i$ affects neither detection nor false alarms under the exact calibration ([proposition D.7](../appendix/appendix.md#prop-capacity)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 4.3 Awareness, theories, and conjectures \{#sec-awareness-sub\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 4.2 (Awareness and expressible theories) \{#def-awareness\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$'s awareness partition is $\\mathcal P_i\\in\\Pi(S)$, the set of partitions of $S$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The agent's represented causal and decision objects are restricted by $\\mathcal P_i$: theories and policies are defined on its cells, conjectures condition only on its cells, and beliefs range only over theories expressible on it.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *expressible theories* are the cell-constant mechanism assignments\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +M(\\mathcal P_i)=\\\{m:\\mathcal P_i\\to\\Theta\\\}, \\tag\{1\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  $$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  with $m$ predicting $x_i\\sim\\marg\{i\}m(C)(a)$ at cell $C$ under profile $a$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Partition $\\mathcal P$ is *sufficient* for an assignment $\\tau'$ if\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\tau'$ is constant on every cell of $\\mathcal P$, equivalently if $\\mathcal\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -P$ refines $\\mathcal P^\{\\tau'\}$; the objective assignment is expressible for\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -agent $i$ if and only if $\\mathcal P_i$ is sufficient for $\\tau$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -An awareness cell is thus an implicit causal claim: the states it pools are\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -alike in how actions produce consequences.  An expressible theory makes the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -claim explicit by naming the mechanism.  Restricting theories to\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -*single* mechanism assignments\u8212?rather than arbitrary conditional\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -distributions per cell\u8212?is substantive and deliberate.  If any distribution\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -were expressible, any within-cell mixture would be too, and no experience\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -could reject the class: coarseness alone cannot produce a detectable failure\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -in a saturated model space.  The mechanism vocabulary $\\Theta$ is known to\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -all agents from the outset; what an agent may lack is the *distinction*\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -that separates states carrying different mechanisms.  Everything the paper\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -calls insight is the production of such a distinction.  Expansion of the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -mechanism vocabulary itself is a deeper dimension of awareness growth\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -deliberately left outside this paper.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Agent $i$ holds a belief $\\mu_i\\in\\Delta(M(\\mathcal P_i))$ over expressible\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theories\u8212?formally, the acting tier of a two-tier belief specified in\par}
