{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Assumption 3.4 (Commitment and subjective optimality) \{#ass-commitment\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -At the start of each generation, agent $i$ commits to a policy\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\sigma_i:\\mathcal P_i\\to A_i$ satisfying\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\\sigma_i(C)\\in\\arg\\max_\{a_i\\in A_i\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Partition $\\mathcal P$ is *sufficient* for an assignment $\\tau'$ if $\\tau'$ is constant on every cell of $\\mathcal P$, equivalently if $\\mathcal P$ refines $\\mathcal P^\{\\tau'\}$; the objective assignment is expressible for agent $i$ if and only if $\\mathcal P_i$ is sufficient for $\\tau$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +An awareness cell is thus an implicit causal claim: the states it pools are alike in how actions produce consequences.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +An expressible theory makes the claim explicit by naming the mechanism.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Restricting theories to *single* mechanism assignments\u8212?rather than arbitrary conditional distributions per cell\u8212?is substantive and deliberate.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If any distribution were expressible, any within-cell mixture would be too, and no experience could reject the class: coarseness alone cannot produce a detectable failure in a saturated model space.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$ holds a belief $\\mu_i\\in\\Delta(M(\\mathcal P_i))$ over $M(\\mathcal P_i)$\u8212?formally, the acting tier of a two-tier belief specified in [section 6](#sec-dissonance)\u8212?and a conjecture $q_i:\\mathcal P_i\\to\\Delta(A_\{-i\})$ about rival play that conditions on $\\mathcal P_i$-cells.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Theories and conjectures are different kinds of objects and are deliberately separated: a theory is a causal claim about the objective system; a conjecture is a strategic claim about what other agents will do.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The diagnostic apparatus developed below tests theories, not conjectures.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Assumption 4.3 (Observation, retention, and the two records) \{#ass-observation\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At the start of each period agent $i$ perceives its current awareness cell $C_\{\\mathcal P_i\}(s)$, the cell of $\\mathcal P_i$ containing $s$; at the end it additionally observes the realized action profile $a$ and its own consequence $x_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Each period is retained as a token.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Two records are distinguished throughout.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *deliberative record* $r_i$ is the multiset of tokens $(C_\{\\mathcal P_i\}(s),a,x_i)$; it is the only record on which the agent's beliefs, tests, and decisions may depend.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *analyst record* $\\bar r_i$ additionally carries the underlying state $s$ of each token.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +No object used in the agent's deliberation may depend on the underlying state labels carried only by $\\bar r_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Analyst-level constructions that require those labels\u8212?in particular the restorative correspondence and the warrant accounting of [section 9](#sec-pivot)\u8212?are so marked.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The non-deliberative representation-producing technology is specified separately in [definition 7.2](#def-technology).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +*Recodability.*\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +When the agent acquires a new distinction\u8212?a refinement $\\mathcal P'$ of its partition\u8212?it also acquires the capacity to classify each retained token under $\\mathcal P'$, producing a recoded deliberative record.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Before acquisition neither belief nor action can condition on that distinction.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This capacity is a modeling idealization: it treats the retained particular as re-describable under any predicate the agent comes to possess, while denying the agent any pre-insight access to the predicate itself.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Assumption 4.4 (Standing policy, subjective optimality, and costly switching) \{#ass-commitment\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$ carries a *standing policy* $\\sigma_i:\\mathcal P_i\\to A_i$ from one period to the next.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Given its current acting belief $\\mu_i$ and conjecture $q_i$, the *belief-optimal* policy $\\sigma_i^\{\\star\}$ plays at each cell $C$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\sigma_i^\{\\star\}(C)\\in\\arg\\max_\{a_i\\in A_i\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \\sum_\{m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)\}\\mu_i(m)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \\sum_\{a_\{-i\}\\in A_\{-i\}\}q_i(a_\{-i\}\\mid C)\\,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim m(C)(a_i,a_\{-i\})\}\\!\\left[u_i(x_i)\\right], \\tag\{3\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -holds it for the generation's $T$ episodes, and revises beliefs, conjectures,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -and policies only between generations.  When dissonance\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -([section 5](#sec-dissonance)) has made $\\mu_i$ undefined and no new representation\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -has been produced, the previously committed policy is carried forward.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Agents evaluate one generation ahead; no discounting is imposed.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Commitment is where statistics and economics meet: a test needs a stable\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -data-generating configuration, and a generation of committed play supplies\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -exactly that.  What a deterministic model must assume as a batching device\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -is, in the stochastic model, the natural granularity of learning.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -### 3.4 On-path conditionals \{#on-path-conditionals\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Fix a generation with committed profile $\\sigma=(\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_n)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Each state $s$ receives the deterministic profile\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$a(s)=(\\sigma_j(C_\{\\mathcal P_j\}(s)))_\{j\\in N\}$.  Agent $i$'s *on-path\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -conditional* at an observation pair $(C,a)$ with\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$S(C,a)=\\\{s\\in C:a(s)=a\\\}\\neq\\emptysetset$ is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim m(C)(a_i,a_\{-i\})\}\\!\\left[\\pi_i(x_i)\\right]. \\tag\{2\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The associated subjective per-period value is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +U_i(\\sigma\\mid\\mu_i,q_i)=\\sum_\{C\\in\\mathcal P_i\}\\rho(C)\\sum_\{m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)\}\\mu_i(m)\\sum_\{a_\{-i\}\\in A_\{-i\}\}q_i(a_\{-i\}\\mid C)\\,\\mathbb E_\{x\\sim m(C)(\\sigma(C),a_\{-i\})\}\\!\\left[\\pi_i(x_i)\\right], \\tag\{3\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +where $\\rho(C)=\\sum_\{s\\in C\}\\rho(s)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$ values a lumpy, one-time cost against the gain it produces over a fixed *valuation horizon* of $T$ periods, common across agents, without discounting\u8212?the planning horizon over which a one-time cost is amortized, a bounded-rationality stand-in for discounting.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Revising the standing policy is costly: agent $i$ replaces $\\sigma_i$ by $\\sigma_i^\{\\star\}$ if and only if the horizon-$T$ gain clears a one-time *switching hurdle* $\\kappa_i\\ge0$,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +T\\bigl(U_i(\\sigma_i^\{\\star\}\\mid\\mu_i,q_i)-U_i(\\sigma_i\\mid\\mu_i,q_i)\\bigr)>\\kappa_i,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +and otherwise carries $\\sigma_i$ unchanged.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The inaction band $T\\,[\\,U_i(\\sigma_i^\{\\star\})-U_i(\\sigma_i)\\,]\\le\\kappa_i$ is the source of policy inertia: beliefs may drift and the belief-optimal action may move, yet the standing policy holds until the gain from switching clears the hurdle.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +When dissonance ([section 6](#sec-dissonance)) leaves $\\mu_i$ undefined, $\\sigma_i^\{\\star\}$ is undefined and the standing policy is carried forward unchanged.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Policy inertia is thus an outcome, not an assumption: a standing policy persists until the belief-optimal action moves far enough to clear the switching hurdle $\\kappa_i$, or until the language itself breaks down and must be repaired ([section 6](#sec-dissonance)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +How long play stays fixed is therefore governed by agent-level parameters\u8212?the switching hurdle here, and the tolerance for misfit introduced with the test of [section 6](#sec-dissonance)\u8212?rather than fixed by assumption.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Figure 2 displays the period timeline.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +> **Figure 2.** The period timeline.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +> Solid steps occur every period: the agent perceives its current awareness cell, acts through its standing policy, observes the realized action profile and its own consequence, and updates its beliefs and sequential test ([assumption 4.3](#ass-observation), [assumption 4.4](#ass-commitment) and [definition 6.1](#def-test)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +> Dashed steps are contingent: dissonance occurs only if the active set empties ([definition 6.3](#def-dissonance)); a dissonant agent elects inquiry only if $\\lambda_i^\{(k_i)\}\\widehat B_i\\ge\\gamma_i$ ([proposition 8.4](#prop-participation)); a successful election refines awareness, and formulation with a bridge prior restores judgment ([section 9](#sec-pivot)); a policy switch occurs only when the horizon-$T$ gain clears the hurdle $\\kappa_i$ ([assumption 4.4](#ass-commitment)).\par}
