{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +## 5. Bayesian competence and its boundary \{#sec-bayesian\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Within fixed awareness, learning is ordinary: the Bayesian belief tier updates by Bayes' rule, while conjectures are updated empirically from observed play.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +[Section 6](#sec-dissonance) distinguishes the reference posterior from the acting belief once test-based exclusions are introduced.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If no agent's awareness ever changes, the model is a repeated interaction among subjectively rational Bayesian learners in the sense of Kalai and Lehrer (1993) and Ryall (2003), and its stable configurations are self-confirming equilibria (Fudenberg and Levine 1993).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Everything new in this paper happens at the boundary of that layer, which two results locate.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### Proposition 5.1 (No Bayesian emergence) \{#prop-no-emergence\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Any well-defined judged or ordinary conditioning of a prior $\\mu\\in\\Delta(M(\\mathcal P_i))$ on an observation in the agent's observation algebra yields a posterior $\\mu'\\in\\Delta(M(\\mathcal P_i))$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +No sequence of such updates makes a theory outside $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ expressible or a distinction finer than $\\mathcal P_i$ usable in a policy.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  #### Proof \{#proof-prop-no-emergence\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Conditioning reweights a distribution on its domain and cannot enlarge the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -domain; every element of $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ is cell-constant, so every\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -posterior mixture, and every policy optimal against one, is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\mathcal P_i$-measurable. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -### Remark 4.2 (The posterior is self-sealing) \{#rem-self-sealing\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -The stochastic setting yields a sharper point.  Suppose every expressible\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theory is false: the agent's cell mixes mechanisms, so no cell-constant\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -assignment matches the on-path conditionals.  A full-support posterior does\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -not signal this.  By the logic of misspecified Bayesian learning\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -(Berk 1966), it concentrates on the expressible theories closest to the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -on-path conditionals in Kullback\u8211?Leibler divergence, and the agent becomes\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -increasingly *confident* in a wrong theory.  Posterior mass always sums\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -to one; the inadequacy of the whole model class is not an event in the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -agent's algebra.  Detecting that the language itself is broken therefore\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -requires a faculty outside the posterior\u8212?the consistency test of the next\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -section.  The distinction matters for how the theory-based view should treat\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\u8220?updating\u8221?: ranking the answers one can express and judging whether any\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -expressible answer is adequate are different cognitive operations, and only\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -the first is Bayesian.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Conditioning reweights a distribution on its domain and cannot enlarge the domain; every element of $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ is cell-constant, so every posterior mixture, and every policy optimal against one, is $\\mathcal P_i$-measurable. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### The posterior is self-sealing \{#rem-self-sealing\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The stochastic setting yields a sharper point.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Suppose the on-path data-generating conditionals lie outside the expressible model class, so every expressible theory is misspecified on path.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A full-support posterior does not signal this.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +By the logic of misspecified Bayesian learning (Berk 1966), it concentrates on the expressible theories closest to the on-path conditionals in Kullback\u8211?Leibler divergence, and the agent becomes increasingly *confident* in one of the least-misspecified expressible theories.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Posterior mass always sums to one; the inadequacy of the whole represented model class is not an event in the agent's algebra.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Diagnosing that no expressible theory is adequate to the observed on-path conditionals therefore requires a faculty outside the posterior\u8212?the consistency test of the next section.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The distinction matters for how the theory-based view should treat \u8220?updating\u8221?: ranking the answers one can express and judging whether any expressible answer is adequate are different cognitive operations, and only the first is Bayesian.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ### Example 1 (continued) \{#example-1-continued\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
