{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -## 5. Causal dissonance \{#sec-dissonance\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -### 5.1 Tests and active sets \{#tests-and-active-sets\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Definition 5.1 (Consistency test and active set) \{#def-test\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Fix a tolerance $\\eta\\geq0$, a minimum count $\\bar n\\geq1$, and a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -nonincreasing slack sequence $w(\\cdot)\\to0$.  Given record $r_i$ with counts\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$n_i(C,a)$ and empirical conditionals $\\hat p_i(\\cdot\\mid C,a)$, theory\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)$ *passes* if\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under the coarse partition, firm $i$'s expressible theories are the constant assignments $m^0$, $m^1$, $m^n$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The row law $\\tau$ is inexpressible: no reweighting of $\\\{m^0,m^1,m^n\\\}$, on any evidence, produces it ([proposition 5.1](#prop-no-emergence)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +After successive deterministic observations eliminate the expressible theories one by one, the observation that eliminates the last theory with positive posterior mass produces a zero Bayesian normalizer, so ordinary conditioning itself is undefined\u8212?the deterministic instance of [\u8220?The posterior is self-sealing\u8221?](#rem-self-sealing), in which misspecification announces itself only because likelihoods hit zero exactly.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With stochastic full-support mechanisms, the Bayesian normalizer remains positive and no such announcement occurs.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +## 6. Causal dissonance \{#sec-dissonance\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 6.1 Tests and active sets \{#tests-and-active-sets\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 6.1 (Sequential adequacy test, dissonance capacity, and active set) \{#def-test\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix an adequacy tolerance $\\eta\\ge0$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Theory $m$ is *adequate* at a visited pair $(C,a)$ when $\\TV\\!\\left(p_i^\{*\}(\\cdot\\mid C,a),\\marg\{i\}m(C)(a)\\right)\\le\\eta$, where $\\TV$ is total-variation distance; under a standing profile call $m$ *$\\eta$-adequate* if it is adequate at every visited pair and *$\\eta$-inadequate* if it is inadequate at some visited pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The agent monitors adequacy *sequentially*, period by period.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For each theory admitted when the current language is initialized, it maintains a nonnegative *evidence process* $E_i^\{m\}$, initialized at $1$ with that language; under adequacy, $E_i^\{m\}$ is a nonnegative supermartingale, while at a recurrent pair where $m$ is strictly inadequate the construction in [appendix A](../appendix/appendix.md#app-eprocess) contains a fixed betting component with strictly positive expected log-growth.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because the total evidence process assigns that component positive weight, $E_i^\{m\}$ diverges almost surely under recurrent strict inadequacy.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$ carries a *dissonance capacity* $b_i>0$ and *rejects* $m$, permanently, at the first period in which $\\log E_i^\{m\}\\ge b_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *active set* $\\widehat M_i(\\mathcal P_i,r_i)$ contains the theories admitted when the current language was initialized that have not subsequently been rejected by the sequential test.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +On the initial language all theories in $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ are admitted and each begins with $E_i^m=1$; after a refinement, admission is determined by the initial repair screen described in the judged-updating paragraph below.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Capacity is the agent's evidence threshold for abandoning a theory: for a theory adequate from initialization, Ville's inequality gives $\\Pr(\\sup_t E_i^m(t)\\ge e^\{b_i\})\\le e^\{-b_i\}$, so a high-capacity agent tolerates apparent misfit and rarely rejects on noise, while a low-capacity agent rejects on slight evidence and suffers more false alarms.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Retrospective fits to a *fixed* record score a candidate against evidence already in hand rather than a stream, using the record's counts $n_i(C,a)$ and empirical conditionals $\\hat p_i(\\cdot\\mid C,a)$ and a nonnegative, nonincreasing slack $w(\\cdot)\\to0$: theory $m$ *fits* at a pair $(C,a)$ when\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  $$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \\TV\\bigl(\\hat p_i(\\cdot\\mid C,a),\\,\\marg\{i\}m(C)(a)\\bigr)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\le\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\eta+w\\bigl(n_i(C,a)\\bigr). \\tag\{5\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The repair test of [section 7](#sec-inquiry) imposes this at every pair with $n_i(C,a)>0$; the grouping test of [section 8](#sec-economics) imposes it wherever $n_i(C,a)\\ge\\bar n$, a minimum count $\\bar n\\ge1$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Sequential testing is consistent: a theory strictly inadequate at a recurrent pair is almost surely rejected, an adequate theory is falsely rejected with conditional probability at most $E_i^m(t_0)e^\{-b_i\}$, and the active set is monotone and eventually constant; the formal statement and proof are [lemma D.1](../appendix/appendix.md#lem-test-consistency) in [appendix D](../appendix/appendix.md#app-deferred).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The inherited-value term matters: a theory adequate under the current profile may carry evidence from an earlier regime, so the active set and its future false-rejection bound track the whole retained process, not current play alone.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under the exact calibration the separate zero-likelihood detector of [appendix A](../appendix/appendix.md#app-eprocess) applies and capacity $b_i$ plays no role ([\u8220?The Calibration Convention\u8221?](#conv-calibration)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Assumption 6.2 (Full-support marginals, stochastic case) \{#ass-full-support\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In the stochastic case every mechanism marginal $\\marg\{i\}\\theta(a)$ has full support on $X_i$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +(The deterministic case, including the running example, is exempt and needs no such assumption.)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The likelihood of a token $(C,a,x_i)$ under theory $m$ is $\\marg\{i\}m(C)(a)(x_i)$: the agent's subjective model treats the $(C,a)$-occurrence probability as theory-independent, so it cancels in Bayes' rule and updating requires neither $\\rho$ nor the within-cell state mixture.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +That this occurrence probability is objectively theory-informative\u8212?rivals' finer play correlates $a_\{-i\}$ with the within-cell mechanism\u8212?is exactly the misspecification that feeds dissonance ([\u8220?The posterior is self-sealing\u8221?](#rem-self-sealing)); the agent does not model it.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Judged updating \{#judged-updating\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Beliefs carry two tiers.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *reference posterior* $\\bar\\mu_i$ begins at a full-support prior on $M(\\mathcal P_i)$ and updates by the token likelihoods above; it is never conditioned on the test.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The *acting belief* $\\mu_i$ is the reference posterior restricted and renormalized to the current active set, so that belief never rides on a rejected theory; when the active set is empty, the acting belief is undefined and the carry-forward clause of [assumption 4.4](#ass-commitment) governs.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because the active set only shrinks after the current language is initialized, the acting belief is well defined at every period until\u8212?if ever\u8212?the last active theory is rejected; [assumption 6.2](#ass-full-support) keeps every reference likelihood positive, so restriction to any nonempty active set is always to a positive-mass set.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In the deterministic case judged updating coincides with ordinary Bayesian conditioning while the Bayesian normalizer is positive; a zero-likelihood contradiction can instead make ordinary conditioning itself undefined.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +After a refinement to $\\mathcal P_i'$, the recoded record supplies an initial repair screen: the new-language active set is initialized as $\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P_i',\\bar r_i)$, which the agent can compute from its recoded deliberative record, and excluded theories begin inactive without replaying the historical record through the sequential evidence process.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Each admitted theory begins with $E_i^m=1$; the reference posterior begins at the full-support bridge prior $\\mu_i^+\\in\\Delta(M(\\mathcal P_i'))$, and the acting belief is its restriction, renormalized on $\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P_i',\\bar r_i)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Thereafter the reference posterior and sequential evidence processes update from newly arriving tokens, permanent sequential rejection further shrinks the active set, and the acting belief is the reference posterior restricted and renormalized to the current active set.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Judged updating is the formal junction between the test and the acting belief\u8212?between judgment and understanding ([\u8220?The posterior is self-sealing\u8221?](#rem-self-sealing))\u8212?and it guarantees, in [section 10](#sec-dynamics), that surviving acting beliefs are supported on adequate theories.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 6.2 Dissonance and exposure \{#dissonance-and-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 6.3 (Causal dissonance) \{#def-dissonance\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Agent $i$ experiences *causal dissonance* at any period at which $\\widehat M_i(\\mathcal P_i,r_i)=\\emptysetset$: no theory admitted on the current language remains unrejected.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The expressible set does not disappear; every element is either excluded by the current language's initialization screen or subsequently rejected by the sequential test.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The diagnostic event is determinate\u8212?no theory admitted on the current language remains\u8212?but its cause and repair need not be.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The modeled question is: *is there an unrepresented distinction among the pooled states that can reconcile the record, and if so what is it?*\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Within the model, dissonance is the endogenous trigger and localization of an anomaly-driven question for intelligence.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +It is localized by the cells and observation pairs at which admitted theories fail, directed because any successful repair must reconcile the retained record, and semantically open because no expressible object names the candidate distinction.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under exact deterministic testing, dissonance implies that the current partition is insufficient for the objective assignment: an expressible true deterministic theory could not be rejected.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In the stochastic model, false rejection and irreparable finite records mean that dissonance is an operational diagnosis, not by itself proof of objective representational insufficiency ([lemma D.1](../appendix/appendix.md#lem-test-consistency) and [remark D.3](../appendix/appendix.md#rem-irreparable)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Whether dissonance occurs depends on whether play exposes inadequacy of the represented class; objective falsity can remain concealed.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 6.4 (Exposure and concealment) \{#def-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under a standing profile, awareness $\\mathcal P_i$ is *exposed* if every $m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)$ is $\\eta$-inadequate.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A standing profile is *concealing* for agent $i$ if some expressible theory is $\\eta$-adequate under it while false as a claim about $\\tau$ at some state its cells cover.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proposition 6.5 (Exposure and dissonance) \{#prop-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix a standing profile and suppose no theory sits exactly at the adequacy boundary ([lemma D.1](../appendix/appendix.md#lem-test-consistency)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If every theory that is $\\eta$-adequate under the fixed profile remains active when that profile begins at $t_0$, then $\\mathcal P_i$ is exposed if and only if eventual dissonance occurs almost surely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If the profile is not exposed and $m$ is an active $\\eta$-adequate theory at entry, then, conditional on the entry history, dissonance is avoided forever with probability at least $1-E_i^m(t_0)e^\{-b_i\}$; if some such $m$ satisfies $E_i^m(t_0)\\le1$, this bound is at least $1-e^\{-b_i\}$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +##### Proof \{#proof-prop-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +By [lemma D.1](../appendix/appendix.md#lem-test-consistency), every entry-active theory strictly inadequate at a recurrent pair is eventually rejected almost surely, while every theory inactive at $t_0$ remains absent.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If all profile-adequate theories are active at $t_0$, the eventual active set is therefore empty almost surely exactly when there is no $\\eta$-adequate theory\u8212?that is, exactly when the profile is exposed.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For a non-exposed profile, conditional Ville's inequality bounds the future rejection probability of any entry-active adequate theory $m$ by $E_i^m(t_0)e^\{-b_i\}$, so the stated survival and no-dissonance bounds follow. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The activity proviso is not cosmetic: the dynamics of [section 10](#sec-dynamics) generate records whose early periods were played under coarser partitions and different profiles, so a terminally adequate theory may already be absent because it was excluded when the current language was initialized or was subsequently rejected, while an active theory may inherit an evidence value above one.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Two recurring mechanisms generate exposure, and the distinction organizes much of what follows.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Heterogeneity inside a cell surfaces either because *other agents play differently across the pooled states*\u8212?strategic differentiation inside one's categories, which changes the visited profiles and the induced mixtures\u8212?or because *the environment punishes uniform play across causally different states in an observable way*.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Both reappear as the corrective forces in [section 10](#sec-dynamics).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Example 1 (continued) \{#example-1-continued-2\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Let $\\beta=1$ and suppose the firms' priors over the constant theories are heterogeneous: firm 1 considers $\\theta^0$ more likely than $\\theta^1$, and firm 2 the reverse.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A direct computation shows that, against any conjecture about the rival, offering architecture $0$ is subjectively strictly dominant for firm 1 and architecture $1$ for firm 2 whenever $\\mu_i(m^a)>(2-\\beta)\\,\\mu_i(m^\{a'\})$; at $\\beta=1$ this is simple belief asymmetry.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The firms therefore adopt differentiated constant policies.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Take the valuation horizon $T=2$ and condition on two periods that serve the diagonal states $s_\{00\}$ and $s_\{11\}$ under differentiated play\u8212?a positive-probability event under uniform $\\rho$; the appendix records how the leverage below scales with the realized composition.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With differentiated actions, the winner-take-all payoffs reveal the objectively preferred architecture at each state: firm 1 wins at $s_\{00\}$ (payoffs $1,0$) and loses at $s_\{11\}$ (payoffs $0,1$).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +No constant assignment is consistent with both observations\u8212?$m^0$ fails at $s_\{11\}$, $m^1$ at $s_\{00\}$, and $m^n$ predicts one-half at both\u8212?so each firm's active set empties: causal dissonance, in its exact deterministic form.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Had the firms instead pooled on the same architecture at both states, each would have received one-half at every period, an outcome consistent with all three constant theories: the pooled profile is concealing, and coarse awareness would have survived its own evidence indefinitely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Belief heterogeneity, by inducing differentiated play, is what made the shared language's failure visible.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +## 7. Inquiry: restoration, direction, and fallibility \{#sec-inquiry\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Within the model, dissonance poses an anomaly-driven question the current language cannot answer.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This section characterizes what a successful answer must accomplish, shows that the requirement directs inquiry without determining its output, and establishes that any inquiry process meeting the paper's disciplines must be fallible.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 7.1 Restorative refinements \{#restorative-refinements\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +For $\\mathcal P,\\mathcal P'\\in\\Pi(S)$, write $\\mathcal P\\prec\\mathcal P'$ when $\\mathcal P'$ strictly refines $\\mathcal P$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The restorative correspondence is an *analyst-level* object: deciding whether a candidate refinement $\\mathcal P'$ restores expressibility requires recoding tokens to $\\mathcal P'$-cells, which uses the analyst record $\\bar r_i$ of [assumption 4.3](#ass-observation).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +It is defined here for the analysis of what a successful answer must accomplish; [section 8](#sec-economics) builds the agent's own deliberative valuation separately.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 7.1 (Restorative refinement) \{#def-restorative\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix a candidate refinement $\\mathcal P_i\\prec\\mathcal P'$ and recode the analyst record $\\bar r_i$ to the deliberative record $r_i^\{\\mathcal P'\}$ on $\\mathcal P'$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Define the *repair-consistent active set*\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P',\\bar r_i)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +=\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\left\\\{\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +m'\\in M(\\mathcal P'):\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +m'\\text\{ satisfies \\cref\{eq:repair-test\}\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right\\\},\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +where the *repair test* is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\TV\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\hat p_i(\\cdot\\mid C',a),\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\marg\{i\}m'(C')(a)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \\leq\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\\eta+w\\bigl(n_i(C,a)\\bigr)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\eta+w\\!\\left(n_i(C',a)\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\quad\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\text\{for every $(C',a)$ with \}n_i(C',a)>0. \\tag\{6\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Here the counts and empirical conditionals are computed from $r_i^\{\\mathcal P'\}$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The refinement $\\mathcal P'$ is *restorative* at $(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)$ if $\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P',\\bar r_i)\\neq\\emptysetset$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +It is *minimally restorative* if no restorative $\\mathcal P''$ satisfies $\\mathcal P_i\\prec\\mathcal P''\\prec\\mathcal P'$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Write $\\mathcal R(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)$ for the set of minimally restorative refinements.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The repair test deliberately applies to every nonempty recoded cell\u8211?profile pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The count floor $\\bar n$ is not part of the repair test; it applies only to the deliberative grouping test of [section 8](#sec-economics).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Every nonempty recoded fragment remains subject to equation (6), while the larger finite-sample slack $w(n)$ at small $n$ preserves the intended caution about sparse evidence.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Refinement can therefore reconcile observations but cannot delete their constraints.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under the exact calibration the repair test is exact consistency at every observed pair ([\u8220?The Calibration Convention\u8221?](#conv-calibration)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Restoration factorizes cell by cell, so finding a repair is a coloring problem; the formal statement and proof are [proposition D.2](../appendix/appendix.md#prop-coherence) in [appendix D](../appendix/appendix.md#app-deferred).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Coherence is the general form of a conflict structure.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Token sets are *mutually incoherent* when no single mechanism accommodates their union; every restorative refinement must separate mutually incoherent sets, since a cell containing both would be incoherent.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +In the deterministic running example incoherence is a binary relation between labeled periods and restoration is a graph-coloring problem; in general, a mixture of three mechanisms may defeat every single mechanism even though each pair of tokens is individually accommodatable, so restoration is a hypergraph coloring.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Two further structural facts matter.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +First, minimal restorations, when they exist, are typically multiple\u8212?as the example shows vividly.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Second, existence itself is guaranteed only in the deterministic case.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With deterministic mechanisms every dissonant record is repairable, and finiteness of $\\Pi(S)$ ensures a minimally restorative refinement exists ([remark D.3](../appendix/appendix.md#rem-irreparable), [appendix D](../appendix/appendix.md#app-deferred)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +With stochastic mechanisms this guarantee fails.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Multiple observations at a single state may yield empirical conditionals for which no one mechanism satisfies equation (6) across the observed profiles.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because no refinement can split a state, such a record has $\\mathcal R(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)=\\emptysetset$ and is *irreparable*: dissonance names a defect that no expressible refinement can repair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Irreparability is an analyst-level property: it is a fact about $\\bar r_i$, and the agent, lacking the state labels, cannot in general tell an irreparable record from a repairable one\u8212?this is precisely the agent's inability to distinguish causal heterogeneity from mechanism noise, developed in [section 8](#sec-economics).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +When the record is irreparable the technology fails surely ([definition 7.2](#def-technology)); the resulting behavior is governed by the deliberative valuation and failure-discouragement of [section 8](#sec-economics).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If the deliberative record still admits a positive-leverage coherent grouping, the agent may elect a doomed inquiry until its achievability weight decays; if no coherent grouping prices the question, it declines immediately.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Neither response requires the agent to recognize analyst-level irreparability.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Under recurring visitation irreparability is typically transient, but a record whose critical pairs stop being visited can remain irreparable forever, a possibility [section 10](#sec-dynamics) classifies within dissonant stasis.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 7.2 The technology \{#the-technology\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 7.2 (Representation-producing technology) \{#def-technology\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A representation-producing technology for agent $i$ is a possibly randomized map $\\phi_i$ from the analyst record $(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)$ to $\\\{\\mathcal P_i\\\}\\cup\\mathcal R(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)$: it fails, returning the current partition, or succeeds, producing a minimally restorative refinement.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +When $\\mathcal R(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)=\\emptysetset$, the range is the singleton $\\\{\\mathcal P_i\\\}$ and the technology fails surely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +It is *truth-blind*: its output distribution depends only on $(\\mathcal P_i,\\bar r_i)$, never directly on $\\tau$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The technology consumes the analyst record because producing a distinction operates on retained particulars in their full concreteness; what the agent lacks pre-insight is not the particulars but the predicate that organizes them, and truth-blindness records that even this concrete operation cannot read off the objective law.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A complexity ordering on refinements\u8212?the analyst's model of which conceptual products the agent's comparison capacities can generate\u8212?may restrict the range further, provided it leaves the admissible subset of $\\mathcal R$ nonempty whenever $\\mathcal R$ is; it is a primitive of the technology, not an object of the agent's optimization.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Definition 7.3 (Truth-blind formulation rule) \{#def-formulation-rule\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +After successful production of $\\mathcal P_i'$, let $r_i^\{\\mathcal P_i'\}$ denote the deliberative record recoded to that partition.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +At this point the agent can compute from $r_i^\{\\mathcal P_i'\}$ the same repair-consistent active set that the analyst writes as $\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P_i',\\bar r_i)$.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A *formulation rule* is a possibly randomized kernel\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\psi_i\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\cdot\\mid\\mathcal P_i',r_i^\{\\mathcal P_i'\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\in\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\Delta\\!\\left(\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\widehat M_i^\{\\,R\}(\\mathcal P_i',\\bar r_i)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\right).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +It is *record-measurable and truth-blind* if its conditional distribution depends only on the displayed arguments and on internal randomness independent of the objective assignment, state draws, consequence draws, and production randomness.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A *truth-blind inquiry-and-formulation rule* is a pair $(\\phi_i,\\psi_i)$ satisfying these restrictions.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Any randomized post-refinement prior-selection rule is subject to the same record-measurability and truth-blindness requirement.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The technology is the paper's stated representational primitive.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +This model does not derive the semantic act that produces a new predicate; it treats that act as a representational technology and shows how experience directs, constrains, prices, and disciplines its output.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The model endogenizes the demand for representational production, the record-dependent constraints on successful products, their unavoidable fallibility, and their strategic consequences, while leaving the internal creative operation primitive.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The output, and even the set of possible outputs, is not a pre-insight subjective menu: the agent cannot name a candidate refinement, assign it a probability, or condition a policy on it before production succeeds ([section 4.6](#sec-commitments)).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +### 7.3 Nonidentification and the price of robustness \{#nonidentification-and-the-price-of-robustness\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Theorem 7.4 (Observational nonidentification) \{#thm-nonidentification\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Fix common initial epistemic states, common record-measurable behavioral and tie-breaking rules, common truth-blind inquiry-and-formulation rules, and a finite horizon of $H$ periods.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Let $\\tau\\neq\\tau'$ be objective assignments whose *joint* consequence laws agree at every state\u8211?profile pair reachable through $H$ under either world from those initial conditions:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\tau(s)(a)=\\tau'(s)(a)\\in\\Delta(X)\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \\qquad\par}
