{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -### 5.2 Dissonance and exposure \{#dissonance-and-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Definition 5.3 (Causal dissonance) \{#def-dissonance\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Agent $i$ experiences *causal dissonance* at the end of a generation if\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\widehat M_i(\\mathcal P_i,r_i)=\\emptysetset$: every expressible theory is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -rejected.  The expressible set does not disappear; no element of it accounts\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -for the record.  The immediate question has a determinate defect but not a\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -determinate answer: *what unrepresented difference among the pooled\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -states explains the heterogeneity of their consequences?*\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Dissonance is the model's rendering of a question for intelligence.  It is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -localized (the failing cells are identified by the test), it is directed (an\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -adequate repair must reconcile the record), and it is semantically open (no\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -expressible object names the missing distinction).  Whether dissonance ever\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -occurs, however, depends on whether play makes the falsity of the language\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -visible.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Definition 5.4 (Exposure and concealment) \{#def-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Under a committed profile, awareness $\\mathcal P_i$ is *exposed* if\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -every $m\\in M(\\mathcal P_i)$ is $\\eta$-inadequate.  A committed profile is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -*concealing* for agent $i$ if some expressible theory is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\eta$-adequate under it while false as a claim about $\\tau$ at some state\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -its cells cover.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Proposition 5.5 (Exposure and dissonance) \{#prop-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Under a fixed committed profile, agent $i$ experiences dissonance from some\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -generation onward almost surely if and only if $\\mathcal P_i$ is exposed.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -##### Proof \{#proof-prop-exposure\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Immediate from [lemma 5.2](#lem-test-consistency): exposure means every expressible\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -theory is eventually rejected; its failure means some theory is eventually\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -always accepted. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Exposure has two sources, and the distinction organizes much of what\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -follows.  Heterogeneity inside a cell surfaces either because *other\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -agents play differently across the pooled states*\u8212?strategic\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -differentiation inside one's categories, which changes the visited profiles\par}
