{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -### 6.3 Nonidentification and the price of robustness \{#nonidentification-and-the-price-of-robustness\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Theorem 6.5 (Observational nonidentification) \{#thm-nonidentification\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Let $\\tau\\neq\\tau'$ be objective assignments inducing the same distribution\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -over agent $i$'s records under the committed profiles played.  Then any\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -truth-blind technology, and any truth-blind inquiry-and-formulation rule,\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -has the same output distribution in both worlds.  If no single theory is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -$\\eta$-adequate under both worlds at the visited pairs, no truth-blind rule\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -selects an adequate formulation with probability one in both.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\\text\{at every such $(s,a)$.\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +$$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The two worlds admit a coupling under which, through $H$, their state draws, full consequence profiles, analyst and deliberative records, partition paths, beliefs, conjectures, standing policies, and production-and-formulation outputs coincide pathwise.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +If the equality holds at every pair reachable at any finite horizon, the coupling extends to the entire process.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Consequently, no truth-blind inquiry-and-formulation rule can produce an objectively correct formulation with probability one in both worlds.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +More generally, the same conclusion holds for any success criterion whose sets of realizable outputs are disjoint across the two worlds.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ##### Proof \{#proof-thm-nonidentification\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -The rule's input is the record, whose distribution is identical by\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -hypothesis, so its output distribution is identical.  If the adequate sets\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -are disjoint, any realized output is inadequate in at least one world with\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -the corresponding probability. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -#### Proposition 6.6 (Robust sufficiency) \{#prop-robust-sufficiency\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -A partition sufficient for every assignment in a candidate set $\\mathcal T$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -must refine $\\bigvee_\{\\tau'\\in\\mathcal T\}\\mathcal P^\{\\tau'\}$, the join of the\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -candidates' causal-equivalence partitions.  A representation guaranteed to\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -accommodate all observationally equivalent worlds is accordingly finer than\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -any single world requires; a technology that economizes on distinctions must\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -accept fallibility.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Use common state draws, common independent random seeds for production, formulation, bridge-prior selection, and tie-breaking, and an identity coupling of the joint consequence draws at every reachable pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Proceed by induction over periods.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Identical histories imply identical current partitions and standing policies, hence the same action profile at the common state.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The pair is reachable, so the two objective assignments give it the same joint consequence distribution and the coupled consequence profiles coincide.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Both analyst and deliberative records therefore remain identical.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Record-measurability and truth-blindness then give identical belief updates, conjectures, policies, refinements, and formulations, closing the induction through $H$; the all-horizons claim follows by countability.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Thus every truth-blind rule has the same output distribution in the two worlds.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Because $\\tau\\neq\\tau'$, their sets of objectively correct formulations are disjoint, so that common distribution cannot assign probability one to both sets.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +The same argument applies to any other disjoint pair of success sets. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +#### Proposition 7.5 (Robust sufficiency) \{#prop-robust-sufficiency\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A partition sufficient for every assignment in a candidate set $\\mathcal T$ must refine $\\bigvee_\{\\tau'\\in\\mathcal T\}\\mathcal P^\{\\tau'\}$, where $\\bigvee$ denotes the coarsest partition that refines every candidate causal-equivalence partition.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +A representation guaranteed to accommodate all observationally equivalent worlds is accordingly finer than any single world requires; a technology that economizes on distinctions must accept fallibility.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  ##### Proof \{#proof-prop-robust-sufficiency\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -Sufficiency for $\\tau'$ is refinement of $\\mathcal P^\{\\tau'\}$\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -([definition 3.2](#def-awareness)); refinement of each candidate's partition is\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -refinement of their join. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +Sufficiency for $\\tau'$ is refinement of $\\mathcal P^\{\\tau'\}$ ([definition 4.2](#def-awareness)); refinement of each candidate's partition is refinement of their join. \u8718?\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
