{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: B_i(r_i) is well defined, computable, invariant across R(P_i, r_i) and nonnegative; the agent elects iff lambda_i B_i(r_i) >= gamma_i, hence only at dissonant records admitting a repair; and B_i(r_i) > 0 iff some pinned rule strictly and robustly improves on the carried-forward policy.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    hypotheses: ["at a dissonant record"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: The record is finite, so it has finitely many groupings; for every coherent group Theta_i(g) is nonempty and finite, and all action and consequence spaces are finite, so every max-min expression and every L_fine, L_cur and J is well defined.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-pinned, paper/paper.md#def-leverage]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: The set whose maximum defines the leverage always contains 0, including when there is no coherent grouping, which gives existence and nonnegativity.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-leverage]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: For fixed C and G, the sum over groups of the maximum of f_\{C,g\} is at least the maximum of the sum, because the left side permits a separate action per intersection; summing over cells gives L_fine >= L_cur and J >= 0.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-leverage]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: Each per-token gain is at most Delta pi_i, so L_fine <= |r_i| Delta pi_i; choosing the standing action in each cell gives L_cur >= 0; hence 0 <= J <= |r_i| Delta pi_i and B_i <= T Delta pi_i.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-leverage, paper/paper.md#ass-commitment, derivations:D2]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: Computability follows by finite enumeration from the deliberative record, the standing policy, the agent-known primitives and the test calibration.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#ass-observation, paper/paper.md#def-test]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: The election rule is the achievability-weighted represented gain net of the inquiry cost.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#ass-elective]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: Because every J is nonnegative and the outside option is zero, the maximum is strictly positive exactly when some coherent grouping has strictly positive representational increment.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-leverage]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    conclusion: B_i is well defined, nonnegative, bounded by T Delta pi_i and computable; the agent elects iff lambda^(k) B_i >= gamma_i; B_i > 0 iff some coherent grouping has J > 0.\par}
