{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +  - id: D18\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    states: appendix/appendix.md#prop-rest-points\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0      hypotheses:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "Assumption 9.3: (i) generic tolerance; (ii) production randomness independent across elections and of the state and consequence draws, with success probability at least pi_lower > 0 conditional on any election"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "for part (d): the committed profile is eventually constant"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (a) Each successful production strictly refines one agent's partition and, by cumulative awareness, no partition ever coarsens; the total cell count is integer valued, starts at no less than n, never exceeds n|S| and strictly increases at every success, so at most n(|S| - 1) successes occur on every path and each agent's partition is eventually constant.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#ass-elective, appendix/appendix.md#part-a, derivations:D5]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (b) Enumerate elections in order with success indicators Z_k; by Proposition 7.4 every election occurs at a dissonant record with R nonempty, so Assumption 9.3(ii) gives conditional success probability at least pi_lower at each election; by Levy's extension of the Borel-Cantelli lemma, on the event of infinitely many elections sum_k Z_k is infinite almost surely, which part (a) bounds by n(|S| - 1); hence infinitely many elections has probability zero.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#prop-participation, paper/paper.md#ass-regularity, appendix/appendix.md#probability-framework, appendix/appendix.md#part-b, "cited: Williams (1991), Theorem 12.15, Levy's extension of the Borel-Cantelli lemma"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (c) Let G* be the larger of the last refinement generation and the last election generation, almost surely finite by (a) and (b); after G* no language changes and no inquiry cost is paid; because the election rule is mechanical, every dissonance episode after G* fails the participation threshold; all other epistemic activity after G* is judged-Bayesian updating within the fixed terminal language.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#prop-participation, paper/paper.md#judged-updating, appendix/appendix.md#part-c]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (d) Condition on the committed profile being eventually constant, equal to sigma* from some generation G_2 >= G*; a frozen pair is visited at most finitely often, so its count, empirical conditional and test constraint are eventually constant; a recurrent pair is visited each episode with probability rho(S_sigma*(C, a)) > 0, so its count diverges and its cumulative empirical conditional converges to p_i* under sigma* by the strong law on the finite space X_i; by Assumption 9.3(i) each theory's distance at a recurrent pair is strictly below or strictly above eta, so with w(n) -> 0 each constraint is eventually permanently satisfied or permanently failed; with finitely many theories and pairs the active set is eventually constant, equal to the set A_i* of theories satisfying every frozen constraint and lying within eta of the terminal on-path conditional at every recurrent pair, whose members are eta-adequate under terminal play at every recurrent pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#probability-framework, paper/paper.md#on-path-conditionals, paper/paper.md#def-test, paper/paper.md#ass-regularity, appendix/appendix.md#part-d, "cited: the strong law of large numbers"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (d, continued) If A_i* is nonempty, agent i is eventually never dissonant; if A_i* is empty, it is eventually always dissonant, and since no election occurs after G* and the election rule is mechanical, lambda_i B_i < gamma_i at every such generation, which includes the case B_i = 0 and in particular irreparable records with R(P_i, r_i) empty.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-dissonance, paper/paper.md#prop-participation, paper/paper.md#rem-irreparable, appendix/appendix.md#part-d]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: Almost surely at most n(|S| - 1) refinements and finitely many elections occur and there is a finite generation after which no language changes and no inquiry cost is paid; on the event that the committed profile is eventually constant, every active set is eventually constant and eta-adequate at recurrent pairs, and each agent is eventually either never dissonant or in dissonant stasis.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -  - id: D15\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    states: paper/paper.md#prop-rest-points\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    hypotheses:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "(i) awareness partitions, committed policies, conjectures and acting beliefs are all eventually constant almost surely"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "(ii) the epistemic states satisfy the SCUE conditions, play generates only records with B_i = 0, and each agent's committed action at each cell is strictly optimal under every conjecture and every theory in its active set"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "(ii-a) deterministic mechanisms"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "(ii-b) stochastic mechanisms; every theory in every active set is eta'-adequate under the configuration's play for some eta' < eta; the strict optimality extends to every (eta + epsilon)-adequate theory for some epsilon in (0, 2(eta - eta')]; the configuration is reached with at least N(delta) observations at every recurrent pair"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (i) Condition (i) of Definition 9.1 holds because each generation's committed policy solves equation (3) at the then-current belief and conjecture, which are eventually the limits.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-i, paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#ass-commitment]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (i) Empirical conjectures converge almost surely to the frequencies induced by the eventually-constant profile, and an eventually-constant conjecture equals its limit, so q_i agrees with induced play at every on-path cell, which is condition (ii).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-i, paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#sec-bayesian]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (i) An agent not in dissonant stasis is eventually never dissonant by Theorem 9.4(d); judged updating keeps the support of the acting belief inside the active set, which is eventually A_i*, whose members are eta-adequate at every recurrent pair, which is condition (iii).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-i, paper/paper.md#thm-absorption, paper/paper.md#judged-updating]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (i) Elections cease by Theorem 9.4(b), so whenever dissonance occurs the participation threshold fails; absent dissonance, B_i = 0 by Definition 7.3, which is condition (iv).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-i, paper/paper.md#thm-absorption, paper/paper.md#def-leverage]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (ii-a) With deterministic mechanisms, SCUE condition (iii) forces every on-path observation to match the active theories exactly, since an active theory's prediction at a visited pair is degenerate and a single disagreeing observation would reject it permanently; so empirical conditionals are constant and active sets never change; dissonance never occurs; B_i = 0 at every generated record gives no election and no refinement; and strict optimality of the committed action under every conjecture and every active theory fixes sigma_i however the reference posterior and the empirical conjectures evolve, so the SCUE conditions continue to hold with conjectures converging to the constant induced play.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-ii-a, paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#def-test, paper/paper.md#ass-elective]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (ii-b) Fix epsilon in (0, 2(eta - eta')] and choose N so that w(n) <= epsilon/2 for all n >= N and the geometric tail sum_\{n >= N\} K e^\{-n epsilon^2/2\} is at most delta/P, K the constant of the finite-alphabet Hoeffding bound and P the number of recurrent pairs across agents; the event that some recurrent pair's empirical conditional ever leaves the epsilon/2-ball around its on-path conditional after count N then has probability at most delta.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-ii-b, derivations:D22, "cited: Hoeffding's inequality, as a union of bounds over the events of the finite space X_i"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: (ii-b) On the complement, every eta'-adequate theory passes the test at every recurrent pair since TV <= epsilon/2 + eta' <= eta, and frozen constraints are unchanged and already satisfied, so active sets remain nonempty and dissonance never occurs; every theory the test admits is (eta + epsilon)-adequate since TV <= eta + w(n) + epsilon/2 <= eta + epsilon, so the extended strict-optimality hypothesis fixes each committed action for every conjecture and every acting belief the judged update can produce; play never changes, the generated records have B_i = 0 so no election or refinement occurs, and the SCUE conditions hold in every subsequent generation with conjectures converging to the constant induced play.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-ii-b, paper/paper.md#def-test, paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#ass-elective, derivations:D22]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: A converging configuration limits, off the agents in dissonant stasis, to a SCUE; a zero-leverage SCUE with strict optimality margins persists, surely under deterministic mechanisms and, under stochastic mechanisms with the stated margins, with probability at least 1 - delta once every recurrent pair carries N(delta) observations.\par}
