{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -  - id: D16\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    states: paper/paper.md#lem-test-consistency\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    hypotheses:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - a committed profile played in all generations\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - "no theory's distance to an on-path conditional equals eta exactly"\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: Visited pairs accumulate observations without bound almost surely under i.i.d. full-support states, so the empirical conditional at each visited pair converges to the on-path conditional by the strong law of large numbers.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#sec-primitives, paper/paper.md#on-path-conditionals, paper/paper.md#def-test, "cited: the strong law of large numbers"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: The triangle inequality places the total-variation distance from the empirical conditional to a theory's prediction within any epsilon > 0 of the distance from the on-path conditional from some generation on.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-test]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: The genericity hypothesis converts strict inequalities into eventual acceptance and rejection, and the slack sequence absorbs finite-sample fluctuation before that generation.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-test]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: Almost surely every eta-adequate theory is eventually always in the active set and every eta-inadequate theory is eventually never in it.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -  - id: D17\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    states: paper/paper.md#prop-coherence\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    hypotheses: ["a refinement P' of P_i, the retained record recoded to its cells"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: "Theories factor across cells: M(P') = Theta^\{P'\}, and the constraints of equation (5) apply cell by cell."\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-awareness, paper/paper.md#def-test]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: So a passing assignment exists exactly when a passing mechanism can be chosen for each cell independently, that is, exactly when every cell is coherent.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-test]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: The recoded active set on P' is nonempty if and only if every cell of P' is coherent.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -  - id: D18\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    states: paper/paper.md#prop-adequacy\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    hypotheses: ["in a SCUE"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: Definition 9.1(iii) supports each acting belief on eta-adequate theories, and the test-consistency lemma makes the surviving active set consist of theories eta-adequate at every visited pair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#lem-test-consistency]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: "The residual clause is the complement of the visited pairs' constraints: falsity survives only at states pooled with causally different states whose mixture imitates a single mechanism at the visited profiles, and in counterfactual claims at unvisited profiles."\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-exposure, paper/paper.md#on-path-conditionals]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: In a SCUE every theory in the support of every acting belief is eta-adequate at every visited pair, and surviving falsity is confined to what the configuration does not expose.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - eventually constant almost surely\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - strict no-switch point at the limit\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - satisfy the SCUE conditions\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - strictly optimal under every conjecture and every theory in its active set\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: (i) Continuity of the finite-dimensional payoff comparison carries the strict no-switch slack to the limit, so the eventually constant policy is stationary under the switching rule; empirical conjectures converge to the induced frequencies; non-stasis agents are eventually never dissonant, with acting beliefs on the eta-adequate survivor set; elections cease. The limit is a partial SCUE.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-i, paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#thm-absorption, paper/paper.md#ass-commitment]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: (ii-a) With deterministic mechanisms rejection is irreversible; SCUE condition (iii) gives an active 0-adequate theory, which is never rejected, so the active set stays nonempty; dissonance never occurs, B_i = 0 gives no election, and strict optimality fixes the policy as the set shrinks and conjectures converge.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-ii-a, paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#conv-calibration]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: (ii-b) Condition on the entry history and couple to the fixed-profile auxiliary process; each entry-active eta-adequate theory with E <= 1 is rejected with probability at most e^\{-b_i\} by Ville's inequality; a union bound over agents leaves probability at least 1 - sum_i e^\{-b_i\} that no agent is ever dissonant; on that event strict optimality fixes play, B_i = 0 stops elections, and the SCUE conditions hold in every period; in the deterministic case surely.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#proof-of-proposition-ii-b, appendix/appendix.md#app-eprocess, derivations:D4, paper/paper.md#def-scue]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    conclusion: Converging non-stasis configurations limit to a partial SCUE, and a zero-leverage SCUE with strict optimality margins persists, exactly under deterministic mechanisms and with probability at least 1 - sum_i e^\{-b_i\} otherwise.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0    - id: D19\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0      kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    states: paper/paper.md#prop-persistence\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    hypotheses: ["the laboratory of Example 1 at beta = 1; the proof is by instance"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: "Instance: firm 1 holds the row refinement and the true assignment and plays the row law; firm 2 remains coarse and plays a constant architecture; the firms pool at two states and differentiate at two, firm 1 winning both, so expected per-generation payoffs are 3/4 and 1/4 under uniform rho, with different terminal partitions and theories."\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#example-1-continued-6, appendix/appendix.md#persistence-payoffs, derivations:D6]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: Firm 2's record rejects all three constant theories (it wins nothing differentiated and loses twice), so it sits in dissonance with B_2 > 0, and it has no priced repair whenever gamma_2 > lambda_2 B_2.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#persistence-payoffs, appendix/appendix.md#unit-leverage, derivations:D2, paper/paper.md#prop-participation]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: The difference is permanent, since firm 1 is never dissonant and firm 2 abstains at every dissonant generation and carries its policy forward, so firm 2 sits in dissonant stasis and the configuration is absorbing.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#example-1-continued-6, paper/paper.md#ass-elective, paper/paper.md#ass-commitment, paper/paper.md#thm-absorption]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: There exist primitives and absorbing configurations with different terminal partitions and theories and strictly, permanently different expected payoffs in which no disadvantaged agent has a priced repair.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    states: appendix/appendix.md#prop-adequacy\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    hypotheses: ["In a SCUE"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: The first claim is condition (iii) of the SCUE definition.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-scue]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: Under strict adequacy, or in the exact deterministic case, the test-consistency lemma makes the active set eventually constant and excludes every eta-inadequate theory; judged updating restricts the acting belief to that set.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#lem-test-consistency, paper/paper.md#judged-updating]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: The residual clause is the complement of the on-path constraints.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-exposure]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    conclusion: In a SCUE acting-belief mass on inadequate theories vanishes, and surviving falsity is confined to what the configuration does not expose.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0  \par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0    - id: D20\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0      kind: proof\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    states: paper/paper.md#prop-diagnostic\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    hypotheses: ["a diagnostic environment (Definition 9.10)"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: By Definition 9.1(iii), theories surviving in a SCUE are not eta-inadequate; in a diagnostic environment every payoff-relevantly false theory is; so no SCUE carries payoff-relevant error.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-scue, paper/paper.md#def-diagnostic]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -      - says: "The second claim restates Definition 5.4: a false theory survives only under concealing profiles, and Proposition 5.5 makes concealment necessary as well as sufficient."\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-exposure, paper/paper.md#prop-exposure]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -    conclusion: In a diagnostic environment every SCUE is free of payoff-relevant error; payoff-relevant falsity survives only in environments that admit concealing profiles and requires them to be sustained.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 -# ----- further closed forms ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    states: appendix/appendix.md#prop-capacity\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    symbols: \{\}\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    hypotheses: ["stochastic mechanisms satisfying"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    steps:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: (i) Exposure makes d_i^m > 0 for every expressible theory and finiteness makes D_i > 0; for each theory choose a recurrent inadequate pair and a positive-weight betting component whose per-period expected log-growth is at least d_i^m / 2.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [paper/paper.md#def-exposure, appendix/appendix.md#app-eprocess]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: Component domination bounds the rejection time by the crossing time of that component's log above b_i minus the log of its weights; bounded log-increments and Wald reasoning give E[tau_m] <= 2(b_i + c_m) / d_i^m; the dissonance time is at most the sum over finitely many theories, so E[tau] = O(b_i / D_i).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#app-eprocess, paper/paper.md#def-test, "cited: Wald's identity for stopping times of processes with bounded increments"]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: Bounded one-period growth of each freshly initialized evidence process means none can cross e^\{b_i\} before a number of periods proportional to b_i, so E[tau] = Omega(b_i).\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#app-eprocess]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +      - says: (ii) is the per-agent bound of the rest-points proposition; neither rate argument applies to the separate exact deterministic detector.\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +        uses: [appendix/appendix.md#prop-rest-points, paper/paper.md#conv-calibration]\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 +    conclusion: Detection time is Theta(b_i) up to the growth rate D_i, and false dissonance at a non-exposed profile has probability at most e^\{-b_i\}.\par}
